{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.star.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# dao.star: Hypermedia Web Applications in datom.world\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design. Mode 1 is specified and buildable on\n     4\t[`dao.stream.http.md`](./dao.stream.http.md) and [`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md).\n     5\tMode 2 is proposed and waits on fenced custody, version 1 of\n     6\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md)\n     7\t(owner ruling 2026-10-07). The published framing is\n     8\t[`daostar-vs-datastar-vs-electric-clojure.blog`](../../public/chp/blog/daostar-vs-datastar-vs-electric-clojure.blog).\n     9\t\n    10\t## Core Philosophy\n    11\t\n    12\t`dao.star` is not a library or an adapter; it is the architectural pattern for building web applications natively in `datom.world`. It emerges from the intersection of two aligned philosophies:\n    13\t1. **datom.world**: Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data flowing over explicit, append-only streams (Axioms 1 and 2). It avoids compiler magic, instead exposing the network explicitly via data.\n    14\t2. **Datastar**: The browser should be a dumb, stateless interpreter of hypermedia (HTML) delivered over Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n    15\t\n    16\tDatastar interprets the SSE projection of an egress `dao.stream`. That is enough to eliminate frontend state management, reactive DAG compilers (like Electric Clojure), and proprietary RPC adapters: standard HTTP in, standard SSE out, and every piece in between is an ordinary stream or an ordinary interpreter.\n    17\t\n    18\t`dao.star` provides two operational modes under one programming model: **The Thin Client** (Arc-style sequential UI) and **The Fat Client** (mobile continuations).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Roles, not nodes.** \"Server\" below means the peer whose composition holds the HTTP boundary; \"browser\" means a peer that can only dial. Neither is a kind of node, and no protocol privilege attaches to either (`datom.world.md`, Design Principles). The HTTP boundary's two media are ordinary streams, so the agent answering requests may run on a peer other than the one that listens, over `dao.stream.remote.md` reflections.\n    21\t\n    22\t---\n    23\t\n    24\t## Mode 1: The Thin Client (Arc-Style Sequential UI)\n    25\t\n    26\tIn the thin client model the browser holds zero business logic and zero shared mutable state. Datastar acts as a dumb terminal rendering HTML patches, while `yin.vm` executes linear, sequential code on the server.\n    27\t\n    28\t### Flow\n    29\t\n    30\t1. **Events In**: A user clicks a button (`data-on-click=\"@post('/k/<token>')\"`). Datastar fires a standard HTTP request. The generic HTTP boundary (`dao.stream.http.md`) mints an exchange id, deposits the request as an unjudged `:http/request` event into the ingress `dao.stream` the composition wired, and holds the socket open. It reads nothing inside the request.\n    31\t2. **Routing**: A router step in the `dao.star` composition reads the ingress with its own cursor and forwards each request to the medium of the continuation the request's token names (*Inputs, order and replay*). The boundary does not route.\n    32\t3. **Resuming**: The continuation's driver, on its next poll, observes the input at its own cursor and steps the parked continuation. Nothing wakes anything; a stream invokes nothing.\n    33\t4. **Semantics and State**: The `yin.vm` agent evaluates business logic linearly. The UI is a derivation of the continuation's state, never stored: the agent projects it into Datastar's SSE wire format (`event: datastar-patch-elements\\ndata: <div>...</div>`) through one pure projection function the composition supplies.\n    34\t5. **Effects Out**: The agent appends to the egress `dao.stream`, tagged with the exchange id: an `:http/head` effect (status, `Content-Type: text/event-stream`), one `:http/chunk` per SSE event with the fully constructed string, and `:http/end` when the response is complete. A long-lived SSE exchange simply never ends.\n    35\t6. **Dumb Socket Routing**: The boundary's `egress-step` observes the egress stream, matches the exchange id to the open socket, and flushes the unmodified payload. Datastar morphs the DOM. The agent parks again, waiting for the next input, for as long as it takes.\n    36\t\n    37\t### First page load\n    38\t\n    39\tA request carrying no token is a request to begin. The router forwards it to the composition's root program, which starts a fresh continuation for the session and answers a complete `text/html` document: `datastar.js`, the first actions carrying the new occurrence's token, and a `data-init=\"@get('/k/<token>')\"` that opens the session's long-lived SSE exchange. From there every request carries a token. A token naming no live occurrence (reclaimed, or after a restart) is answered the same way: begin again.\n    40\t\n    41\t### Signals\n    42\t\n    43\tA Datastar request carries the client's signals: in the `datastar` query parameter for GET and DELETE, in the JSON body otherwise. The boundary deposits them where HTTP put them, inside `:http/query` or `:http/body`, unread.\n    44\t\n    45\tDecoding is interpretation. `dao.star` supplies one pure projection from a request event to a signals map. The ingress tuple gains no slot for it, and signals become datoms only when an application interpreter derives them from the retained ingress stream (`datom.world.md`, Derive, don't persist).\n    46\t\n    47\tSignals are client-held state that moves, not shared state: each request carries an immutable snapshot, and the agent changes them only by appending a `datastar-patch-signals` effect. They are also the client's unverified claim. A signal is input to a continuation, never its state and never authority.\n    48\t\n    49\t### Inputs, order and replay\n    50\t\n    51\t**A request names its occurrence.** Each projected UI carries, in its action path, the token of the parked occurrence that rendered it. The token is unguessable, is the request's only routing and idempotence key, and until ShiBi is load-bearing is the sole gate on Mode 1 input: a pre-shared value in `dao.shibi.md`'s sense (owner ruling 2026-10-07). The boundary never reads it.\n    52\t\n    53\t**Order is the ingress position.** Two requests for one occurrence are not concurrent once deposited: one is earlier. Custody decides who consumes it. In a one-process composition the session's single driver is the only resumer; that exclusion is process-scoped and says so. Where more than one peer observes the ingress, resumption is a take, and a take is a lease on the occurrence (UCF §7.7).\n    54\t\n    55\t**One input, one successor.** Consuming an input advances occurrence `O` to its successor `O'`. The reply goes to the exchange that carried the consumed request; patches the session should see on its long-lived exchange go there too, the open exchanges of a session being derived from ingress events, not kept.\n    56\t\n    57\t**Stale input is one-shot** (owner ruling 2026-10-07). A double click, a retry, a replayed URL, a second tab or the back button names an `O` that already has a successor. It runs nothing. It is answered by projecting the current occurrence again and ending the exchange. The UI is a derivation, so answering twice is harmless, and a retry whose first reply was lost still converges.\n    58\t\n    59\t**Multi-shot is opt-in.** A composition may declare a continuation `:yin.k/fork`, Arc's behaviour: a stale input then resumes a sibling branch, and its effects are at-least-once.\n    60\t\n    61\t**Effects.** Exactly-once needs a fenced writer into an enrolled consumer (UCF §7.7.5). Every other stream is at-least-once and declared so.\n    62\t\n    63\t### Liveness\n    64\t\n    65\tThree resources, kept apart. None adds a clock to the wait for input: a parked continuation's `next` on its input is `blocked` until a request arrives, on a Tuesday or a Thursday.\n    66\t\n    67\t**The open exchange is leased.** The judge runs inside the peer that holds the HTTP boundary. For each `:http/request` it observes it grants unsolicited on a lease medium it writes: subject `{:http/exchange x}`, holder the egress medium's identity, a duration in `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Reclaim is the boundary's own close of that exchange, idempotent; the adapter deposits `:http/closed`, then the judge records `:lapsed`. A responder that keeps an exchange open observes its grant and appends `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal :dao.lease/lease L}` to the egress medium at under half the duration, from its own control flow, against its own tick stream. The judge and `egress-step` are peer observers of that medium; each ignores the other's facts. A one-shot responder answers inside the granted duration and renews nothing. An exchange nobody answers lapses by `:silence`; Datastar's reconnect opens a new one.\n    68\t\n    69\t**Keep-alive is the responder's.** On its tick it may append an `:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the wire, so the host discovers a dead peer by writing; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the other. A peer's close or a failed write is deposited as `:http/closed`; the responder reads it as data and stops, and the judge ends the exchange lease with cause `:policy`.\n    70\t\n    71\t**The parked continuation is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07). A parked occurrence nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor; when a continuation parks on occurrence `O` the judge grants unsolicited, subject `{:dao.star/parked O}`, holder the session agent, duration the composition's idle allowance. The holder releases when `O` is consumed, and while the session's long-lived exchange is open it renews from its tick, so a connected session is retained and a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. Reclaim retracts the parked record (taking is a write), after which the token names nothing and the next request begins again. The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\n    72\t\n    73\tSizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval; the keep-alive interval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path; a boundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so its inventory to reclaim is empty. A false lapse is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    74\t\n    75\t---\n    76\t\n    77\t## Mode 2: The Fat Client (Mobile Continuations via UCF)\n    78\t\n    79\tStatus: proposed. Everything in this section waits on UCF version 1, fenced custody with a `dao.space` transactor as arbitration authority (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.7, 7.7.8). Version 0 is fork-only and cannot offer the exclusivity below.\n    80\t\n    81\tWhile `dao.star` champions the dumb terminal, it is not locked into it. Because `yin.vm` continuations are pure data, they can cross the network boundary to act as SPA-style fat clients for low-latency local interaction.\n    82\t\n    83\t### Flow\n    84\t\n    85\t1. **Exporting**: A `yin.vm` continuation on the server parks and enters the exporting state (UCF §7.7.4): its wait set is detached, so no poll or local resume can perform an effect while the value is in other hands. The lift produces a UCF handoff body, canonical and content-addressed, and the driver records the offer on the arbitration medium.\n    86\t2. **Handoff**: The SSE patch carries the notice, not the body (*Handoff carrier*). A browser-side adapter deposits the notice on a browser-local stream that the browser's `yin.vm` reads; Datastar hands nothing to anyone.\n    87\t3. **Custody**: The browser `yin.vm` fetches the body by address over its WebSocket channel, proposes on the arbitration medium, and resumes only on observing its grant. Custody is exclusive because the lease is: the state moves to the browser rather than being shared with it, and the server's copy stays fenced.\n    88\t4. **Transparent Remote Access**: A continuation that needs a server-held stream uses the portable descriptors it carried and `attach!`es a reflection over WebSockets. Descriptors carry no authorization: the served entries are gated and leased for the migrated continuation (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 6 and 7). On channel loss a reflection answers `channel-gone` and reattachment is the continuation's own step on the same descriptor; a reclaimed entry answers `not-found` and is gone, not disconnected.\n    89\t5. **Migration Back**: The continuation re-serializes itself as a successor and offers it to the server, which admits it under *Admitting a continuation back*.\n    90\t\n    91\t### Handoff carrier\n    92\t\n    93\t**Architect ruling, not yet confirmed by the owner (2026-10-07).** The SSE patch carries a notice — the body's content address, the arbitration descriptor and the channel descriptor — and the body travels by address over the browser's WebSocket channel.\n    94\t\n    95\tReasons: the body is canonical CBOR and SSE is text, so inlining means a second encoding; the egress medium is shared, and a body on it discloses the store slice to every observer, where a notice discloses a name; large values travel as content addresses (`dao.stream.md`, Granularity); and the browser `yin.vm` needs the channel anyway for custody and remote access, so the carrier adds no path.\n    96\t\n    97\tRejected: the body base64-encoded inside a `datastar-patch-signals` or element attribute (double encoding, disclosure on egress, Datastar's payload size); a second HTTP GET by address (a second boundary path for what the channel already carries).\n    98\t\n    99\t### Local projection\n   100\t\n   101\tDeferred with Mode 2. The shape: the browser `yin.vm` appends the same Datastar-format effects to a browser-local egress stream, and a browser-side boundary adapter interprets them into DOM patches. Same effect vocabulary, different boundary; neither the agent nor Datastar learns which one it is behind. Whether that adapter is a Datastar plugin or a direct morph is implementation.\n   102\t\n   103\t### Admitting a continuation back\n   104\t\n   105\tA continuation returning from the browser is admitted as untrusted code and state. It ran where nothing can attest to it, so it is treated as the controlled mode of `dao.space.security.md` treats any pushed interpreter.\n   106\t\n   107\t1. **Custody first** (UCF §7.7, unchanged). The successor must name, in `:yin.k/origin`, the lease the ledger holds live for that holder, and arrive on the holder's own medium. Otherwise `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n   108\t2. **Validate as hostile bytes** (UCF §7.8, lower steps 1 to 4), under a size bound and a step budget the composition declares.\n   109\t3. **Rebind authority; never read it from the value.** Lower into an isolated store. Primitives and FFI ops resolve from the receiver's registry, restricted to the set the composition delegated to this lineage at export. A stream marker attaches only if its identity is in that delegated set, or its channel is the returning peer's own. Anything else is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it.\n   110\t4. **No check survives the trip.** A decision the program made before export may have been skipped by a forged frame. Whatever must hold after return is enforced by a gate on a stream, or stays home in a continuation that never left and answers the migrated one over streams.\n   111\t\n   112\tThe test: a returned continuation can do nothing its principal could not do directly through the gated streams it was given.\n   113\t\n   114\t**Export is disclosure.** The lifted store slice and values are readable by the browser and by every peer that can fetch the address. The composition inspects the computed closure before publishing.\n   115\t\n   116\t**Until effect handlers honor a delegated set** (`dao.space.security.md`, Honest Limits), migration back is admitted from allow-listed peers only (owner ruling 2026-10-07). ShiBi later plugs in at the same gates.\n   117\t\n   118\t---\n   119\t\n   120\t## Design Invariants Maintained\n   121\t\n   122\t- **No Shared Mutable State**: The UI is a pure projection (Mode 1), or custody of the continuation is held under an exclusive lease (Mode 2). State is never shared across the boundary; it moves, and the mover is fenced.\n   123\t- **Multiplexed Correlation**: Connections are not mapped 1:1 to streams. The HTTP boundary observes a shared egress stream and routes by exchange id. The cost is shared: an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, and a composition under that pressure shards the egress.\n   124\t- **Host Isolation**: The HTTP boundary is a dumb pipe that knows nothing of Datastar or `yin.vm` syntax. Datastar is a dumb renderer that knows nothing of backend state. Under Mode 2 a `yin.vm` runs beside it, not inside it.\n   125\t- **Data > Macros**: Compile-time slicing (Electric Clojure) is rejected. Network boundaries are crossed dynamically at runtime via reified data on explicit streams.\n   126\t- **No callbacks, no implicit control flow**: every arrival is a deposit, every departure is an effect, and every wait is a `blocked` answer at a cursor.\n   127\t\n   128\t## Completion Criteria\n   129\t\n   130\t1. **Thin Client**: A `yin.vm` agent receives a Datastar request through the HTTP boundary's ingress stream, answers with `:http/head`, SSE-formatted `:http/chunk`s and `:http/end` on the egress stream, parks, and resumes on the next request for its token. Also: a first load with no token begins a session; a stale token is answered by re-projection; a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`; a silent exchange lapses and closes; a parked continuation nobody returns to lapses and is retracted.\n   131\t2. **Fat Client Handoff**, conditional on UCF version 1 landing: a `yin.vm` agent exports under exclusive custody, the SSE patch carries the notice, a CLJS `yin.vm` in the browser fetches the body by address, obtains the grant, resumes, and reaches a server-held stream through a gated, leased reflection. Migration back from an allow-listed peer is admitted under *Admitting a continuation back*. The carrier shape is an architect ruling pending the owner's confirmation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1791309600000-architect-dao-star-apply-rulings.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-07 06:15:00 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 13:15:00 +07\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude\n     4\tSession-ID: 06daf2e5-59b2-4d67-9c9e-94995df5c027\n     5\t\n     6\tBoth files written in the worktree; nothing staged or committed. `docs/orchestrator-log.md` was already modified before I started and I did not touch it. (The brief's `Created-GMT` header is again a day off from its own local time.)\n     7\t\n     8\t## What changed\n     9\t\n    10\t**`docs/design/dao.stream.http.md`** (new, 131 lines): the Gap 1 ruling as a stream-level spec, subordinate to `dao.stream.md`, sibling of `dao.stream.ws.md`. Exchange-as-attachment with incarnation-bearing ids; `:http/request` / `:http/ended` / `:http/closed` events with exactly one terminal event per exchange; deposit admission reused from ws (`:portable-values`, `:http/max-open`, `:http/max-body-bytes`); `egress-step` performing `:http/head` / `:http/chunk` / `:http/end` in stream order, ignoring values that are not its own, appending no per-effect outcome, holding no clock, exposing exchange close as the reclaim procedure; observably-gone rules per cause; the no-privileged-node paragraph; a Deferred list (HTTP channel for `dao.stream.remote`, streamed bodies, TLS/CORS). It names neither Datastar, SSE nor `yin.vm`.\n    11\t\n    12\t**`docs/design/dao.star.md`** (rewritten in place, 51 → 131 lines):\n    13\t- Status block: Mode 1 buildable, Mode 2 waits on UCF v1 (Q1), correct UCF doc name, blog link.\n    14\t- Core Philosophy: \"Datastar interprets the SSE projection of an egress stream\" replaces \"speaks the universal language of streams\"; \"Backend/Frontend\" labels dropped; new **Roles, not nodes** paragraph.\n    15\t- Mode 1 flow: routing step added, \"wakes\" replaced by the driver's poll, `:http/head`/`:http/chunk`/`:http/end` effects, long-lived exchange stated.\n    16\t- New **First page load** (glm addition, ruled: token-less request → root program → full HTML with `data-init` opening the SSE exchange; an unknown token begins again).\n    17\t- New **Signals** (Gap 2), **Inputs, order and replay** (Gap 5, one-shot default per Q3, token as sole gate per Q5), **Liveness** (Gap 3, plus the parked-continuation lease per Q4 with subject `{:dao.star/parked O}`, holder the session agent, released on consumption, renewed while the session's SSE exchange is open, reclaim = retraction).\n    18\t- Mode 2 marked proposed; flow rewritten with exporting state, custody by lease, gated/leased reflections, honest channel-loss outcomes; new **Handoff carrier** (Q6, marked as architect ruling unconfirmed), **Local projection** (glm addition, explicitly deferred with the shape stated), **Admitting a continuation back** (Gap 4, allow-listed peers per Q2).\n    19\t- Design Invariants corrected (custody by lease, egress-gap cost, \"Datastar is a dumb renderer\", no-callback line added); Completion Criteria rewritten with negative criteria, criterion 2 conditional on UCF v1.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Q6 ruling (architect, owner has not confirmed)\n    22\t\n    23\tThe SSE patch carries a **notice** (content address, arbitration descriptor, channel descriptor); the handoff **body travels by address over the browser's WebSocket channel**. Reasons: the body is canonical CBOR and SSE is text (inlining is a second encoding); the egress medium is shared, so a body on it discloses the store slice to every observer while a notice discloses only a name; `dao.stream.md` Granularity already sends large values as content addresses; the browser `yin.vm` needs the channel anyway for custody and remote access. Rejected: base64 body inside a `datastar-patch-signals` or element attribute (double encoding, egress disclosure, payload size); a second HTTP GET by address (a second boundary path for what the channel already carries). Completion criterion 2 changed accordingly and flagged.\n    24\t\n    25\t## Orchestrator readings\n    26\t\n    27\tAll six readings match my understanding. One note on Q4: the owner said \"dao.lease should be used to expire parked continuations\"; I made the session agent the holder and let the open SSE exchange drive renewals so a connected session is retained and a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. That holder choice is my design, not the owner's words.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Not resolved\n    30\t\n    31\t- The parked-continuation lease is process-scoped until a composition settles `dao.lease.md`'s three durable duties; the blog's \"paused for weeks\" therefore needs a durable judge and store, which no doc yet specifies.\n    32\t- glm's note that the blog shares the \"waking\" locution: the blog is published and the brief says not to contradict it, so I left the blog alone and corrected only the design doc.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.http.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream HTTP Boundary\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement; sibling of [`dao.stream.ws.md`](./dao.stream.ws.md), whose\n     6\tdeposit model and admission rule it reuses. Every sentence below is a rule.\n     7\t[`dao.star.md`](./dao.star.md) is its first consumer and composes it; this\n     8\tdocument knows nothing of Datastar, SSE, or `yin.vm`.\n     9\t\n    10\t## What it is\n    11\t\n    12\tThe HTTP boundary is not a transport and owns no stream. It is an adapter\n    13\t(events in) and a host effect interpreter (effects out) over two ordinary\n    14\tmedia the composition supplies: an **ingress** medium it deposits into and\n    15\tan **egress** medium it reads. It has no reader surface, no `create!`, and\n    16\thands no handle upward. It never reads a request body, a query string or a\n    17\tresponse chunk. Because nothing host-local crosses either medium, both are\n    18\t`:portable-values`, and the interpreter answering requests may sit on any\n    19\tpeer (see *No privileged node*).\n    20\t\n    21\t## Exchange\n    22\t\n    23\tOne request and its response are one attachment; a connection is not\n    24\t(HTTP/1.1 keep-alive and HTTP/2 multiplexing are connection facts below\n    25\tthe transform). The boundary mints `:http/exchange` under the contract's\n    26\tserver-minted attachment rule (`dao.stream.md`, Envelopes): plain data,\n    27\tunique among the exchanges it can distinguish, and never reused for the\n    28\tlifetime of the egress medium. It carries the boundary's incarnation, so a\n    29\tstale effect retained from before a restart can never match a new socket.\n    30\t\n    31\t## Events in\n    32\t\n    33\tWire framing, header parsing and size limits happen below the transform;\n    34\tan over-limit or malformed request is answered there (400, 413, 503) and is\n    35\tnot an event. Every valid request is deposited unjudged:\n    36\t\n    37\t```clojure\n    38\t{:http/exchange x\n    39\t :http/event    :http/request\n    40\t :http/method   :get                 ; a keyword, lower-case\n    41\t :http/path     \"/action\"\n    42\t :http/query    \"…\"                  ; the raw query string, or absent\n    43\t :http/headers  {\"accept\" \"…\" …}     ; lower-cased names, string values\n    44\t :http/body     <bytes>}             ; absent when the request has none\n    45\t\n    46\t{:http/exchange x :http/event :http/ended}    ; an :http/end effect completed\n    47\t{:http/exchange x :http/event :http/closed}   ; every other end\n    48\t```\n    49\t\n    50\tExactly one terminal event is deposited per exchange, after its request\n    51\tevent, whatever the cause: the peer closed, a write failed, an effect was\n    52\tout of order, the lease judge of the composition reclaimed it, the boundary\n    53\tstopped. `:http/ended` is reserved for a response the boundary completed by\n    54\tperforming an `:http/end` effect.\n    55\t\n    56\t## Deposit admission\n    57\t\n    58\tAs `dao.stream.ws.md`, Deposit Admission. The constructor takes the\n    59\tingress destination and its admission declaration as data: evict-oldest\n    60\tretention with a declared capacity, value domain `:portable-values`, able\n    61\tto carry every envelope above, and a lifetime longer than the boundary.\n    62\tWiring a reject-mode destination is a host assembly defect refused at\n    63\tassembly. The constructor also takes `:http/max-open`, the number of\n    64\texchanges held open at once, and `:http/max-body-bytes`; a request beyond\n    65\teither is answered below the transform and never deposited.\n    66\t\n    67\t## Effects out\n    68\t\n    69\t`egress-step` is a step the composition drives at its own cadence, as it\n    70\tdrives `endpoint-step`. It holds one cursor on the egress medium, minted at\n    71\t`:dao.stream/newest` before the listener starts, and performs effects in\n    72\tstream order:\n    73\t\n    74\t```clojure\n    75\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/head  :http/status 200 :http/headers {…}}\n    76\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/chunk :http/body <string-or-bytes>}\n    77\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/end}\n    78\t```\n    79\t\n    80\t`:http/head` is sent once and first; `:http/chunk` writes bytes, a string\n    81\tas UTF-8, and flushes; `:http/end` completes the response and the\n    82\texchange. The step appends no per-effect outcome. A value that is not an\n    83\teffect for an open exchange is not this interpreter's and is ignored: the\n    84\tegress medium has no privileged reader, and other interpreters may share\n    85\tit. The writer learns that an effect was not performed from the exchange's\n    86\tterminal event on the ingress medium, which precedes any effect the\n    87\tboundary ignored for it. An effect out of order for an open exchange — a\n    88\tchunk before the head, a second head, anything after end — closes that\n    89\texchange.\n    90\t\n    91\tThe step takes no clock and holds no timer. How long a silent exchange is\n    92\theld open is the composition's, through `dao.lease.md`; the step exposes\n    93\tthe exchange close as the idempotent reclaim procedure a judge is composed\n    94\twith.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Observably gone\n    97\t\n    98\tA boundary that cannot deposit or cannot write makes itself observably\n    99\tgone by closing a resource it itself holds, the same action for every\n   100\tcause (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces):\n   101\t\n   102\t- a non-`ok` ingress deposit closes that exchange's socket with no\n   103\t  synthesized response; the terminal event has nowhere to land, which is\n   104\t  the contract's irreducible corner;\n   105\t- a failed socket write closes the exchange and deposits `:http/closed`;\n   106\t- an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, since the step cannot say\n   107\t  whose effects were lost, then adopts the recovery cursor;\n   108\t- an egress `end` or `transport-error` closes every open exchange and the\n   109\t  listener: the boundary can never answer again.\n   110\t\n   111\tIt invokes nothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing.\n   112\t\n   113\t## No privileged node\n   114\t\n   115\tWhich end accepted is an establishment fact: HTTP has a requester and a\n   116\tresponder because TCP has a dialer and an acceptor, and a browser can only\n   117\tdial (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 1). The ingress and egress media are\n   118\tordinary streams, so a peer that cannot listen may run the answering\n   119\tinterpreter against reflections of them served by a reachable peer that\n   120\truns only this boundary. The boundary records no role beyond the exchange.\n   121\t\n   122\tHosts: clj (http-kit is already a dependency of the WebSocket transport),\n   123\tcljs on Node, cljd. A browser has no listener.\n   124\t\n   125\t## Deferred\n   126\t\n   127\t- An HTTP channel for `dao.stream.remote.md` (request maps by POST, answers\n   128\t  by a long-lived response) for browsers that cannot open a WebSocket.\n   129\t- Request bodies as streams rather than one bounded value.\n   130\t- TLS termination, CORS and compression: below the transform, unspecified\n   131\t  here.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1791307819601-architect-dao-star-gaps.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1791307819601-architect-dao-star-gaps.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-06 17:40:00 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 00:40:00 +07\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude\n     4\tSession-ID: 06daf2e5-59b2-4d67-9c9e-94995df5c027\n     5\t\n     6\t# dao.star design gaps: rulings\n     7\t\n     8\tRead-only pass; no files edited. The timestamps above are estimates from a `date` call at the start of the pass. The brief's `Created-GMT` is a day ahead of its own `Created-Local`.\n     9\t\n    10\tVerdict: the thin-client shape is sound and matches the blog. The doc has four architectural defects: the response head is unspecified, exclusive custody is asserted rather than obtained, migration back has no admission rule, and nothing routes or orders requests. The rest is wording or deferred work.\n    11\t\n    12\t## Gap 1: HTTP boundary\n    13\t\n    14\t**Ruling:** it needs its own doc, `docs/design/dao.stream.http.md`, subordinate to `dao.stream.md` and sibling to `dao.stream.ws.md`. `dao.star.md` cites it and specifies only the composition. The boundary is generic; specifying it inside `dao.star.md` would make a stream-level contract depend on a document above it.\n    15\t\n    16\t**Rejected:**\n    17\t- **ws-style per-exchange writer handle through a handoff slot.** A live handle forces `:host-values` media and pins the agent to the boundary's process. Correlated effects keep both media `:portable-values`, so the agent can sit on any peer.\n    18\t- **Boundary writes the SSE headers itself.** It would then know SSE.\n    19\t- **One egress medium per exchange.** It contradicts the doc's and blog's shared-egress framing; a composition may still shard.\n    20\t\n    21\tPaste-ready (seed of the new doc; `dao.star.md` keeps the first paragraph plus a link):\n    22\t\n    23\t````markdown\n    24\t## The HTTP boundary\n    25\t\n    26\tThe HTTP boundary is not a transport and owns no stream. It is an adapter\n    27\t(events in) and a host effect interpreter (effects out) over two ordinary\n    28\tmedia the composition supplies. It has no reader surface, no `create!`, and\n    29\thands no handle upward. It never reads a request body, a query string or a\n    30\tresponse chunk.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Exchange.** One request and its response are one attachment; a connection\n    33\tis not. The boundary mints `:http/exchange` under the contract's\n    34\tserver-minted attachment rule: plain data, never reused for the lifetime of\n    35\tthe egress medium (it carries the boundary's incarnation), so a stale effect\n    36\tcan never match a new socket.\n    37\t\n    38\t**Events in.** Framing, header parsing and size limits happen below the\n    39\ttransform; an over-limit or malformed request is answered there (400, 413,\n    40\t503) and is not an event. Every valid request is deposited unjudged:\n    41\t\n    42\t```clojure\n    43\t{:http/exchange x :http/event :http/request\n    44\t :http/method :get :http/path \"/action\" :http/query \"…\"\n    45\t :http/headers {…} :http/body <bytes>}\n    46\t{:http/exchange x :http/event :http/ended}    ; an :http/end effect completed\n    47\t{:http/exchange x :http/event :http/closed}   ; every other end\n    48\t```\n    49\t\n    50\tExactly one terminal event is deposited per exchange.\n    51\t\n    52\t**Admission.** The constructor takes the deposit destination and its\n    53\tdeclaration as data: evict-oldest, a declared capacity, `:portable-values`,\n    54\ta lifetime longer than the boundary. A reject-mode destination is a host\n    55\tassembly defect. `:http/max-open` and `:http/max-body-bytes` are composition\n    56\tdata.\n    57\t\n    58\t**Effects out.** `egress-step` is a step the composition drives. It holds\n    59\tone cursor on the egress medium, minted at `:dao.stream/newest` before the\n    60\tlistener starts, and performs, in stream order per exchange:\n    61\t\n    62\t```clojure\n    63\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/head :http/status 200 :http/headers {…}}\n    64\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/chunk :http/body <string-or-bytes>}\n    65\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/end}\n    66\t```\n    67\t\n    68\tA value that is not an effect for an open exchange is not this\n    69\tinterpreter's and is ignored; the egress medium has no privileged reader.\n    70\tThe step appends no per-effect outcome. The writer learns an effect was not\n    71\tperformed from the exchange's terminal event on the ingress medium. An\n    72\teffect out of order for an open exchange (a chunk before the head, a second\n    73\thead) closes that exchange.\n    74\t\n    75\t**Observably gone.** A non-`ok` deposit closes that exchange's socket with\n    76\tno synthesized response. An egress `gap` closes every open exchange, since\n    77\tthe step cannot say whose effects were lost, and adopts the recovery cursor.\n    78\tAn egress `end` closes every exchange and the listener. A failed socket\n    79\twrite closes the exchange. Each is the same action, closing a resource the\n    80\tboundary itself holds.\n    81\t\n    82\t**No privileged node.** Which end accepted is an establishment fact. Both\n    83\tmedia are ordinary streams, so a peer that cannot listen can run the agent\n    84\tagainst reflections of them (`dao.stream.remote.md`) while a reachable peer\n    85\truns only this boundary.\n    86\t````\n    87\t\n    88\t## Gap 2: Datastar signals\n    89\t\n    90\t**Ruling:** opaque payload at the boundary, derived by an interpreter above it. They are never datoms in the ingress tuple and never discarded.\n    91\t\n    92\t**Rejected:**\n    93\t- **Datoms in the ingress tuple.** The boundary would have to know the `datastar` parameter and its JSON, and it would persist structure a projection derives.\n    94\t- **Discarded.** That is filtering at the depositing layer, which Host Boundaries forbids.\n    95\t\n    96\t````markdown\n    97\t### Signals\n    98\t\n    99\tA Datastar request carries the client's signals: in the `datastar` query\n   100\tparameter for GET and DELETE, in the JSON body otherwise. The boundary\n   101\tdeposits them where HTTP put them, inside `:http/query` or `:http/body`,\n   102\tunread.\n   103\t\n   104\tDecoding is interpretation. `dao.star` supplies one pure projection from a\n   105\trequest event to a signals map. The ingress tuple gains no slot for it, and\n   106\tsignals become datoms only when an application interpreter derives them from\n   107\tthe retained ingress stream.\n   108\t\n   109\tSignals are client-held state that moves, not shared state: each request\n   110\tcarries an immutable snapshot, and the agent changes them only by appending\n   111\ta `datastar-patch-signals` effect. They are also the client's unverified\n   112\tclaim. A signal is input to a continuation, never its state and never\n   113\tauthority.\n   114\t````\n   115\t\n   116\t## Gap 3: Liveness\n   117\t\n   118\t**Ruling:** the open exchange is a leased resource. The judge is composed inside the peer that holds the HTTP boundary and reclaims by closing the socket. A parked continuation's wait for input has no deadline.\n   119\t\n   120\t**Rejected:**\n   121\t- **A boundary idle timeout.** It is a switch, not a lease; a long-lived SSE stream is legitimately idle.\n   122\t- **Counting egress chunks as renewals.** `dao.lease.md`: no machinery renews on the holder's behalf.\n   123\t- **Boundary-authored keep-alive bytes.** The boundary would know SSE.\n   124\t\n   125\t````markdown\n   126\t### Liveness\n   127\t\n   128\tThree leases-or-not, kept apart:\n   129\t\n   130\t- **The wait for input has no deadline.** A parked continuation's `next` on\n   131\t  its input is `blocked` until a request arrives, on a Tuesday or a\n   132\t  Thursday. Nothing here adds a clock to it.\n   133\t- **The open exchange is leased.** The judge runs inside the peer that holds\n   134\t  the HTTP boundary. For each `:http/request` it observes, it grants\n   135\t  unsolicited on a lease medium it writes: subject `{:http/exchange x}`,\n   136\t  holder the egress medium's identity, a duration in `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`.\n   137\t  Reclaim is the boundary's own close of that exchange, idempotent; the\n   138\t  adapter deposits `:http/closed`, then the judge records `:lapsed`.\n   139\t- **Custody of a migrated continuation** is the UCF lease on its occurrence\n   140\t  (Mode 2). A lapsed socket never ends it.\n   141\t\n   142\t**Renewal.** A responder that keeps an exchange open observes its grant and\n   143\tappends `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal :dao.lease/lease L}` to the\n   144\tegress medium at under half the duration, from its own control flow, against\n   145\tits own tick stream. The judge and `egress-step` are peer observers of that\n   146\tmedium: each ignores the other's facts. A one-shot responder answers inside\n   147\tthe granted duration and renews nothing.\n   148\t\n   149\t**Closed socket.** A peer's FIN or a failed write is deposited as\n   150\t`:http/closed`. The responder reads it as data and stops; the judge ends the\n   151\tlease with cause `:policy`. A dead peer the host has not noticed is\n   152\tdiscovered only by writing.\n   153\t\n   154\t**Keep-alive** is therefore the responder's: on its tick it may append a\n   155\t`:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the\n   156\twire; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the\n   157\tother.\n   158\t\n   159\t**No reply.** An exchange nobody answers lapses by `:silence` and its socket\n   160\tcloses. Datastar's reconnect opens a new exchange. A false lapse is\n   161\tpossible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n   162\t\n   163\tSizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval, and the keep-alive\n   164\tinterval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path. A\n   165\tboundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so the inventory to\n   166\treclaim is empty.\n   167\t````\n   168\t\n   169\t## Gap 4: Fat-client trust\n   170\t\n   171\t**Ruling:** confinement with no amplification. A continuation returning from the browser is an untrusted program submitted by the browser's principal. Custody proves who returned it and that it is the only one; it proves nothing about what the browser did.\n   172\t\n   173\t**Rejected:**\n   174\t- **A signature or MAC over the exported value.** The successor is a different value.\n   175\t- **Replaying the browser's steps to verify.** It defeats the mode.\n   176\t- **Pinning code to the exported segments as the safety argument.** A forged frame can resume at any safepoint past a check.\n   177\t\n   178\t````markdown\n   179\t### Admitting a continuation back\n   180\t\n   181\tA continuation returning from the browser is admitted as untrusted code and\n   182\tstate. It ran where nothing can attest to it, so it is treated as the\n   183\tcontrolled mode of `dao.space.security.md` treats any pushed interpreter.\n   184\t\n   185\t1. **Custody first** (UCF §7.7, unchanged). The exporting peer names an\n   186\t   arbitration medium it trusts in the value. The successor must name, in\n   187\t   `:yin.k/origin`, the lease the ledger holds live for that holder, and\n   188\t   arrive on the holder's own medium. Otherwise `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n   189\t2. **Validate as hostile bytes** (UCF §7.8 lower, steps 1 to 4), under a\n   190\t   size bound and a step budget the composition declares.\n   191\t3. **Rebind authority; never read it from the value.** Lower into an\n   192\t   isolated store. Primitives and FFI ops resolve from the receiver's\n   193\t   registry, restricted to the set the composition delegated to this\n   194\t   lineage at export. A stream marker attaches only if its identity is in\n   195\t   that delegated set, or its channel is the returning peer's own. Anything\n   196\t   else is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it.\n   197\t4. **No check survives the trip.** A decision the program made before export\n   198\t   may have been skipped by a forged frame. Whatever must hold after return\n   199\t   is enforced by a gate on a stream, or stays home in a continuation that\n   200\t   never left and answers the migrated one over streams.\n   201\t\n   202\tThe test: a returned continuation can do nothing its principal could not do\n   203\tdirectly through the gated streams it was given.\n   204\t\n   205\t**Export is disclosure.** The lifted store slice and values are readable by\n   206\tthe browser and by every observer of the carrier. The composition inspects\n   207\tthe computed closure before publishing.\n   208\t\n   209\t**Until ShiBi is load-bearing**, the principal is a channel attachment\n   210\tidentity on an allow-list or a pre-shared value (`dao.shibi.md`). ShiBi\n   211\tlater plugs in at the same gates.\n   212\t\n   213\t**Limit.** Effect handlers that honor a delegated set are not built\n   214\t(`dao.space.security.md`, Honest Limits). Until they are, migration back is\n   215\tenabled only from allow-listed peers.\n   216\t````\n   217\t\n   218\t## Gap 5: Ordering and idempotence\n   219\t\n   220\t**Ruling:** a request is an input to an occurrence, not a resumption. The ingress position orders inputs, one input advances one occurrence, and a stale input gets a re-projection.\n   221\t\n   222\t**Rejected:**\n   223\t- **Deduplicating on the exchange id.** A retry is a new exchange.\n   224\t- **A lock or session table in the boundary.** That is hidden state, and it is interpretation in the pipe.\n   225\t- **Queueing inputs for an occurrence held elsewhere.** It is a silent wait.\n   226\t\n   227\t````markdown\n   228\t### Inputs, order and replay\n   229\t\n   230\t**A request names its occurrence.** Each projected UI carries, in its action\n   231\tpath, the token of the parked occurrence that rendered it. The token is\n   232\tunguessable and is the request's only routing and idempotence key. The\n   233\tboundary does not read it. A router step in the `dao.star` composition does,\n   234\tforwarding each request to the medium its continuation reads.\n   235\t\n   236\t**Order is the ingress position.** Two requests for one occurrence are not\n   237\tconcurrent once deposited: one is earlier. Custody decides who consumes it.\n   238\tIn a one-process composition the session's single driver is the only\n   239\tresumer, and that exclusion is process-scoped and says so. Where more than\n   240\tone peer observes the ingress, resumption is a take, and a take is a lease\n   241\ton the occurrence (UCF §7.7).\n   242\t\n   243\t**One input, one successor.** Consuming an input advances occurrence `O` to\n   244\tits successor `O'`. The reply goes to the exchange that carried the consumed\n   245\trequest.\n   246\t\n   247\t**Stale input.** A double click, a retry, a replayed URL, a second tab or\n   248\tthe back button names an `O` that already has a successor. It runs nothing.\n   249\tIt is answered by projecting the current occurrence again and ending the\n   250\texchange. The UI is a derivation, so answering twice is harmless, and a\n   251\tretry whose first reply was lost still converges.\n   252\t\n   253\t**Multi-shot is opt-in.** A composition may declare a continuation\n   254\t`:yin.k/fork`, Arc's behaviour: a stale input then resumes a sibling branch,\n   255\tand its effects are at-least-once.\n   256\t\n   257\t**Effects.** Exactly-once needs a fenced writer into an enrolled consumer\n   258\t(UCF §7.7.5). Every other stream is at-least-once and declared so.\n   259\t\n   260\t**Held elsewhere.** While a browser holds custody of `O`, the exporting\n   261\tpeer's copy is fenced. A request naming `O` is stale. If the browser's lease\n   262\tlapses, the epoch advances, the last recorded occurrence may be granted\n   263\tagain, and the browser's late effects are refused by epoch.\n   264\t````\n   265\t\n   266\t## Defects in the existing text\n   267\t\n   268\tClass A is an architectural defect; class D is deferred work or wording.\n   269\t\n   270\t| Severity | File:line | Invariant / evidence | Correction |\n   271\t|---|---|---|---|\n   272\t| High, A | `docs/design/dao.star.md:23-24`, `:45` | Host Isolation as the doc states it. No effect carries status or `Content-Type`, and nothing ends the response. Either the boundary knows SSE or the browser gets no event stream. | Add `:http/head` and `:http/end` effects (Gap 1). The agent authors the head. |\n   273\t| High, A | `docs/design/dao.star.md:35`, `:43` | UCF §7.1: \"emission transfers nothing\". §7.7.3: without a `dao.space` transactor as authority a composition is fork-only. As written, the exporter's parked record and the browser are both runnable (§7.7.1). | \"Custody is obtained by a lease on the occurrence; the exporter enters the exporting state before publishing.\" Keep \"state moves, not shared\". |\n   274\t| High, A | `docs/design/dao.star.md:37` | `dao.space.security.md` confinement. The receiver would lower attacker-controlled frames with its ambient authority. | Gap 4 section. |\n   275\t| High, A | `docs/design/dao.star.md:21` | Nothing says which continuation a request reaches or what a duplicate does. \"Wakes\" is implicit control flow: a stream invokes nothing. | Gap 5 section. \"The driver's next poll observes it at the agent's cursor.\" |\n   276\t| Medium, A | `docs/design/dao.star.md:20` | `dao.lease.md`: an unbounded held socket is a possessed resource with no reclaim. Deposit destination and admission are undeclared. | Gap 3 section; cite the boundary doc. The correlation id is the attachment identity. |\n   277\t| Medium, A | `docs/design/dao.star.md:34` | The shared egress medium discloses the store slice to every observer. \"Instructs the browser to hand the payload\" is a call across a host boundary. | The patch carries the body's content address and descriptors; the body travels by address. A browser adapter deposits the notice on a browser-local stream that `yin.vm` reads. |\n   278\t| Medium, A | `docs/design/dao.star.md:36` | `dao.stream.remote.md` 2.4 and §6: channel loss is `channel-gone` and reattachment is the caller's; a reclaimed lease is gone, not disconnected. \"Parks until the connection returns\" implies an implicit wait. | State both outcomes. Entries served for a migrated continuation are leased and gated. |\n   279\t| Medium, D | `docs/design/dao.star.md:6-7`, `:17`, `:36`, `:51` | `datom.world.md`: client and server are roles, not kinds of node. \"datom.world (Backend)\" is also false under Mode 2. No protocol privilege is introduced. | Say once: \"server\" means the peer whose composition holds the HTTP boundary; \"browser\" means a peer that can only dial. |\n   280\t| Medium, D | `docs/design/dao.star.md:9` | Datastar holds no handle or cursor and cannot re-observe. It interprets an SSE projection of an egress stream. | \"Datastar interprets the SSE projection of an egress stream.\" Drop \"speaks the universal language of streams\". |\n   281\t| Medium, D | `docs/design/dao.star.md:30`, `:51` | UCF is \"Proposed / Deferred\"; the r5 amendment states no version-1 code is landed. | Cite the status; make criterion 2 conditional on it. |\n   282\t| Low, D | `docs/design/dao.star.md:33-34` | A UCF value is a content-addressed handoff body, not an `[e a v t m]` datom. | \"UCF value\". The blog's looser wording can stand. |\n   283\t| Low, D | `docs/design/dao.star.md:45` | Under Mode 2 the browser runs `yin.vm`. | \"Datastar is a dumb renderer.\" |\n   284\t| Low, D | `docs/design/dao.star.md:44` | A shared egress `gap` closes every open exchange. | State the consequence; allow sharding. |\n   285\t| Low, D | `docs/design/dao.star.md:50` | The order \"receives, suspends, responds\" is wrong, and there is no negative criterion. | Add: a closed socket is observed as data; a stale request re-projects; a silent exchange lapses. |\n   286\t\n   287\tThe six invariants hold once these are applied. Nothing in the blog is contradicted: the head effect, the lease and the token are additions it is silent on.\n   288\t\n   289\t## Open questions for the owner\n   290\t\n   291\t1. **Mode 2 before UCF version 1?** Exclusive custody needs the fenced-custody grammar and a `dao.space` transactor. May Mode 2 ship first as a fork-only demo with the wording changed, or does it wait?\n   292\t2. **Migration back before confinement exists?** I recommend allow-listed peers only until effect handlers honor a delegated set. The alternative is to leave it out of scope entirely.\n   293\t3. **Default for a stale request.** I recommend one-shot with re-projection. The blog invokes Arc, whose continuations were multi-shot, so the back button would re-run them. Which is the default?\n   294\t4. **Abandoned parked continuations.** The blog promises weeks. Who reclaims them and when? This is OD-4 in `dao.stream.md`, still open.\n   295\t5. **Browser principal before ShiBi.** Is the unguessable occurrence token, as a pre-shared value, an acceptable sole gate for Mode 1 input?\n   296\t6. **The handoff body's carrier.** I ruled that the patch carries an address and the body travels over the browser's WebSocket channel. Completion criterion 2 says the state is serialized \"into a Datastar SSE patch\". Confirm that the criterion may change.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
