{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1791309600000-architect-dao-star-apply-rulings.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-07 06:15:00 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 13:15:00 +07\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude\n     4\tSession-ID: 06daf2e5-59b2-4d67-9c9e-94995df5c027\n     5\t\n     6\tBoth files written in the worktree; nothing staged or committed. `docs/orchestrator-log.md` was already modified before I started and I did not touch it. (The brief's `Created-GMT` header is again a day off from its own local time.)\n     7\t\n     8\t## What changed\n     9\t\n    10\t**`docs/design/dao.stream.http.md`** (new, 131 lines): the Gap 1 ruling as a stream-level spec, subordinate to `dao.stream.md`, sibling of `dao.stream.ws.md`. Exchange-as-attachment with incarnation-bearing ids; `:http/request` / `:http/ended` / `:http/closed` events with exactly one terminal event per exchange; deposit admission reused from ws (`:portable-values`, `:http/max-open`, `:http/max-body-bytes`); `egress-step` performing `:http/head` / `:http/chunk` / `:http/end` in stream order, ignoring values that are not its own, appending no per-effect outcome, holding no clock, exposing exchange close as the reclaim procedure; observably-gone rules per cause; the no-privileged-node paragraph; a Deferred list (HTTP channel for `dao.stream.remote`, streamed bodies, TLS/CORS). It names neither Datastar, SSE nor `yin.vm`.\n    11\t\n    12\t**`docs/design/dao.star.md`** (rewritten in place, 51 → 131 lines):\n    13\t- Status block: Mode 1 buildable, Mode 2 waits on UCF v1 (Q1), correct UCF doc name, blog link.\n    14\t- Core Philosophy: \"Datastar interprets the SSE projection of an egress stream\" replaces \"speaks the universal language of streams\"; \"Backend/Frontend\" labels dropped; new **Roles, not nodes** paragraph.\n    15\t- Mode 1 flow: routing step added, \"wakes\" replaced by the driver's poll, `:http/head`/`:http/chunk`/`:http/end` effects, long-lived exchange stated.\n    16\t- New **First page load** (glm addition, ruled: token-less request → root program → full HTML with `data-init` opening the SSE exchange; an unknown token begins again).\n    17\t- New **Signals** (Gap 2), **Inputs, order and replay** (Gap 5, one-shot default per Q3, token as sole gate per Q5), **Liveness** (Gap 3, plus the parked-continuation lease per Q4 with subject `{:dao.star/parked O}`, holder the session agent, released on consumption, renewed while the session's SSE exchange is open, reclaim = retraction).\n    18\t- Mode 2 marked proposed; flow rewritten with exporting state, custody by lease, gated/leased reflections, honest channel-loss outcomes; new **Handoff carrier** (Q6, marked as architect ruling unconfirmed), **Local projection** (glm addition, explicitly deferred with the shape stated), **Admitting a continuation back** (Gap 4, allow-listed peers per Q2).\n    19\t- Design Invariants corrected (custody by lease, egress-gap cost, \"Datastar is a dumb renderer\", no-callback line added); Completion Criteria rewritten with negative criteria, criterion 2 conditional on UCF v1.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Q6 ruling (architect, owner has not confirmed)\n    22\t\n    23\tThe SSE patch carries a **notice** (content address, arbitration descriptor, channel descriptor); the handoff **body travels by address over the browser's WebSocket channel**. Reasons: the body is canonical CBOR and SSE is text (inlining is a second encoding); the egress medium is shared, so a body on it discloses the store slice to every observer while a notice discloses only a name; `dao.stream.md` Granularity already sends large values as content addresses; the browser `yin.vm` needs the channel anyway for custody and remote access. Rejected: base64 body inside a `datastar-patch-signals` or element attribute (double encoding, egress disclosure, payload size); a second HTTP GET by address (a second boundary path for what the channel already carries). Completion criterion 2 changed accordingly and flagged.\n    24\t\n    25\t## Orchestrator readings\n    26\t\n    27\tAll six readings match my understanding. One note on Q4: the owner said \"dao.lease should be used to expire parked continuations\"; I made the session agent the holder and let the open SSE exchange drive renewals so a connected session is retained and a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. That holder choice is my design, not the owner's words.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Not resolved\n    30\t\n    31\t- The parked-continuation lease is process-scoped until a composition settles `dao.lease.md`'s three durable duties; the blog's \"paused for weeks\" therefore needs a durable judge and store, which no doc yet specifies.\n    32\t- glm's note that the blog shares the \"waking\" locution: the blog is published and the brief says not to contradict it, so I left the blog alone and corrected only the design doc.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.http.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream HTTP Boundary\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement; sibling of [`dao.stream.ws.md`](./dao.stream.ws.md), whose\n     6\tdeposit model and admission rule it reuses. Every sentence below is a rule.\n     7\t[`dao.star.md`](./dao.star.md) is its first consumer and composes it; this\n     8\tdocument knows nothing of Datastar, SSE, or `yin.vm`.\n     9\t\n    10\t## What it is\n    11\t\n    12\tThe HTTP boundary is not a transport and owns no stream. It is an adapter\n    13\t(events in) and a host effect interpreter (effects out) over two ordinary\n    14\tmedia the composition supplies: an **ingress** medium it deposits into and\n    15\tan **egress** medium it reads. It has no reader surface, no `create!`, and\n    16\thands no handle upward. It never reads a request body, a query string or a\n    17\tresponse chunk. Because nothing host-local crosses either medium, both are\n    18\t`:portable-values`, and the interpreter answering requests may sit on any\n    19\tpeer (see *No privileged node*).\n    20\t\n    21\t## Exchange\n    22\t\n    23\tOne request and its response are one attachment; a connection is not\n    24\t(HTTP/1.1 keep-alive and HTTP/2 multiplexing are connection facts below\n    25\tthe transform). The boundary mints `:http/exchange` under the contract's\n    26\tserver-minted attachment rule (`dao.stream.md`, Envelopes): plain data,\n    27\tunique among the exchanges it can distinguish, and never reused for the\n    28\tlifetime of the egress medium. It carries the boundary's incarnation, so a\n    29\tstale effect retained from before a restart can never match a new socket.\n    30\t\n    31\t## Events in\n    32\t\n    33\tWire framing, header parsing and size limits happen below the transform;\n    34\tan over-limit or malformed request is answered there (400, 413, 503) and is\n    35\tnot an event. Every valid request is deposited unjudged:\n    36\t\n    37\t```clojure\n    38\t{:http/exchange x\n    39\t :http/event    :http/request\n    40\t :http/method   :get                 ; a keyword, lower-case\n    41\t :http/path     \"/action\"\n    42\t :http/query    \"…\"                  ; the raw query string, or absent\n    43\t :http/headers  {\"accept\" \"…\" …}     ; lower-cased names, string values\n    44\t :http/body     <bytes>}             ; absent when the request has none\n    45\t\n    46\t{:http/exchange x :http/event :http/ended}    ; an :http/end effect completed\n    47\t{:http/exchange x :http/event :http/closed}   ; every other end\n    48\t```\n    49\t\n    50\tExactly one terminal event is deposited per exchange, after its request\n    51\tevent, whatever the cause: the peer closed, a write failed, an effect was\n    52\tout of order, the lease judge of the composition reclaimed it, the boundary\n    53\tstopped. `:http/ended` is reserved for a response the boundary completed by\n    54\tperforming an `:http/end` effect.\n    55\t\n    56\t## Deposit admission\n    57\t\n    58\tAs `dao.stream.ws.md`, Deposit Admission. The constructor takes the\n    59\tingress destination and its admission declaration as data: evict-oldest\n    60\tretention with a declared capacity, value domain `:portable-values`, able\n    61\tto carry every envelope above, and a lifetime longer than the boundary.\n    62\tWiring a reject-mode destination is a host assembly defect refused at\n    63\tassembly. The constructor also takes `:http/max-open`, the number of\n    64\texchanges held open at once, and `:http/max-body-bytes`; a request beyond\n    65\teither is answered below the transform and never deposited.\n    66\t\n    67\t## Effects out\n    68\t\n    69\t`egress-step` is a step the composition drives at its own cadence, as it\n    70\tdrives `endpoint-step`. It holds one cursor on the egress medium, minted at\n    71\t`:dao.stream/newest` before the listener starts, and performs effects in\n    72\tstream order:\n    73\t\n    74\t```clojure\n    75\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/head  :http/status 200 :http/headers {…}}\n    76\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/chunk :http/body <string-or-bytes>}\n    77\t{:http/exchange x :http/effect :http/end}\n    78\t```\n    79\t\n    80\t`:http/head` is sent once and first; `:http/chunk` writes bytes, a string\n    81\tas UTF-8, and flushes; `:http/end` completes the response and the\n    82\texchange. The step appends no per-effect outcome. A value that is not an\n    83\teffect for an open exchange is not this interpreter's and is ignored: the\n    84\tegress medium has no privileged reader, and other interpreters may share\n    85\tit. The writer learns that an effect was not performed from the exchange's\n    86\tterminal event on the ingress medium, which precedes any effect the\n    87\tboundary ignored for it. An effect out of order for an open exchange — a\n    88\tchunk before the head, a second head, anything after end — closes that\n    89\texchange.\n    90\t\n    91\tThe step takes no clock and holds no timer. How long a silent exchange is\n    92\theld open is the composition's, through `dao.lease.md`; the step exposes\n    93\tthe exchange close as the idempotent reclaim procedure a judge is composed\n    94\twith.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Observably gone\n    97\t\n    98\tA boundary that cannot deposit or cannot write makes itself observably\n    99\tgone by closing a resource it itself holds, the same action for every\n   100\tcause (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces):\n   101\t\n   102\t- a non-`ok` ingress deposit closes that exchange's socket with no\n   103\t  synthesized response; the terminal event has nowhere to land, which is\n   104\t  the contract's irreducible corner;\n   105\t- a failed socket write closes the exchange and deposits `:http/closed`;\n   106\t- an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, since the step cannot say\n   107\t  whose effects were lost, then adopts the recovery cursor;\n   108\t- an egress `end` or `transport-error` closes every open exchange and the\n   109\t  listener: the boundary can never answer again.\n   110\t\n   111\tIt invokes nothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing.\n   112\t\n   113\t## No privileged node\n   114\t\n   115\tWhich end accepted is an establishment fact: HTTP has a requester and a\n   116\tresponder because TCP has a dialer and an acceptor, and a browser can only\n   117\tdial (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 1). The ingress and egress media are\n   118\tordinary streams, so a peer that cannot listen may run the answering\n   119\tinterpreter against reflections of them served by a reachable peer that\n   120\truns only this boundary. The boundary records no role beyond the exchange.\n   121\t\n   122\tHosts: clj (http-kit is already a dependency of the WebSocket transport),\n   123\tcljs on Node, cljd. A browser has no listener.\n   124\t\n   125\t## Deferred\n   126\t\n   127\t- An HTTP channel for `dao.stream.remote.md` (request maps by POST, answers\n   128\t  by a long-lived response) for browsers that cannot open a WebSocket.\n   129\t- Request bodies as streams rather than one bounded value.\n   130\t- TLS termination, CORS and compression: below the transform, unspecified\n   131\t  here.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoLease\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implemented (`src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc`, `test/dao/lease_test.cljc`,\n     4\t`test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc`; merged 2026-09-20), derived from and\n     5\tsubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). The three use cases in\n     6\t[`dao.lease.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.lease.implementation-plan.md) are\n     7\tsketches, not finished components. This document is the operative contract:\n     8\tevery sentence below is a rule. Why the design is this shape is in\n     9\t[`dao.lease.rationale.md`](./dao.lease.rationale.md), which binds nothing.\n    10\t\n    11\tA lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed. It adds no operation to any\n    12\tcontract and no key to any DaoStream result map, leaving\n    13\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) unchanged by its existence.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Vocabulary\n    16\t\n    17\tFacts are plain data on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys:\n    18\t`:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence. A\n    19\treader switches on both. A fact carrying neither is not a lease fact and is\n    20\tignored; a fact carrying both is defective.\n    21\t\n    22\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    23\t| Fact     | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Identity carried                            | Also required                                                             |\n    24\t+==========+=======================+=========+=============================================+===========================================================================+\n    25\t| Proposal | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional, an ask              |\n    26\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    27\t| Grant    | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, plus                    | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`;         |\n    28\t|          |                       |         | `:dao.lease/proposal` if answering one      | `:dao.lease/max` optional                                                 |\n    29\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    30\t| Refusal  | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | —                                                                         |\n    31\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    32\t| Release  | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | —                                                                         |\n    33\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    34\t| Reclaim  | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | `:dao.lease/cause` — `:silence`, `:release`, `:cap`, or `:policy`         |\n    35\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    36\t\n    37\t+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n    38\t| Evidence   | `:dao.lease/event`   | Author  | Identity carried     | Also required        |\n    39\t+============+======================+=========+======================+======================+\n    40\t| Renewal    | `:dao.lease/renewal` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`   | —                    |\n    41\t+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n    42\t| Tick       | `:dao.lease/tick`    | adapter | —                    | `:dao.lease/reading` |\n    43\t+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n    44\t\n    45\t**Keys.**\n    46\t\n    47\t- `:dao.lease/lease` — the lease's identity, minted by the grantor, carried by\n    48\t  every fact about it, never reused within that grantor.\n    49\t- `:dao.lease/proposal` — a proposal's identity, minted by the holder, echoed\n    50\t  by the grant or refusal answering it.\n    51\t- `:dao.lease/subject` — what is leased, in whatever plain data the domain uses.\n    52\t- `:dao.lease/holder` — the party whose renewals count, in whatever form the\n    53\t  medium's attribution takes.\n    54\t- `:dao.lease/duration` — a single-entry map from a unit keyword to a positive\n    55\t  integer magnitude. `:dao.lease/max`, `:dao.lease/reading` and a judge's\n    56\t  tolerance use the same representation; tolerance may be zero. A magnitude\n    57\t  stays within the bound that keeps every comparison and sum of the\n    58\t  composition's units exact on every host — the reference bound is 2^52 raw\n    59\t  and, against a supplied table, `quot 2^52 unit-magnitude`; a magnitude past\n    60\t  it establishes nothing.\n    61\t- `:dao.lease/cause` — why a reclaim happened.\n    62\t\n    63\t**Units.** The unit keywords and their ratios are a composition's\n    64\tinteroperability decision, fixed once and shared by its grantor, holders and\n    65\tjudge. Comparison normalizes to the finer of two units, and is strict: an\n    66\tinterval exactly equal to its bound has not yet passed it.\n    67\t\n    68\t**Validity.** A fact is defective, and establishes nothing, when it omits a key\n    69\tits status or event requires; carries a status or event outside the tables\n    70\tabove; carries a duration, cap or reading that is not a single-entry map of a\n    71\tfixed unit to a positive integer; carries a lease id on a proposal; answers one\n    72\tproposal with both `:accepted` and `:rejected`, or repeats `:accepted`,\n    73\t`:released` or `:lapsed` for one lease; or carries a reading older than one\n    74\talready observed. A judge reading a defective fact has read no lease fact.\n    75\t\n    76\t## Authority\n    77\t\n    78\t- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n    79\t  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\n    80\t  evidence and counts as such.\n    81\t- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n    82\t  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n    83\t  measured as though it had not arrived.\n    84\t- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\n    85\t  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\n    86\t- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\n    87\t  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\n    88\t  nothing in this vocabulary.\n    89\t- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\n    90\t  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\n    91\t- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\n    92\t  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\n    93\t  ends the lease.\n    94\t\n    95\t## Prohibitions\n    96\t\n    97\t- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n    98\t  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n    99\t- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n   100\t- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n   101\t- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n   102\t- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n   103\t\n   104\t## Time\n   105\t\n   106\tTime reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\n   107\tclock. Both read a tick stream their composition wires. Readings on one stream\n   108\tnever decrease, and a composition persisting a ledger across a restart keeps\n   109\tits readings comparable across it.\n   110\t\n   111\tA tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\n   112\tunder *Judging with incomplete evidence*.\n   113\t\n   114\t## The judge\n   115\t\n   116\tThe judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\n   117\treclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\n   118\tresource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\n   119\tinterpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\n   120\tinterpreter performing its own act.\n   121\t\n   122\t**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n   123\t\n   124\t- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\n   125\t- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\n   126\t  changes;\n   127\t- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\n   128\t  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\n   129\t- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\n   130\t- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n   131\t  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n   132\t\n   133\tA lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\n   134\tand **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n   135\tappended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\n   138\tas a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\n   139\t\n   140\t### The pass\n   141\t\n   142\tA pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\n   143\tin this order:\n   144\t\n   145\t1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\n   146\t   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n   147\t   **now** for the whole pass.\n   148\t2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n   149\t   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\n   150\t   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n   151\t   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n   152\t   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n   153\t   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\n   154\t   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\n   155\t   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\n   156\t   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n   157\t   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n   158\t   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n   159\t3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n   160\t   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n   161\t   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n   162\t4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n   163\t   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n   164\t   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n   165\t   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n   166\t   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n   167\t   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n   168\t   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n   169\t   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\n   170\t   resumed reading.\n   171\t5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n   172\t   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n   173\t   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n   174\t   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n   175\t6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n   176\t   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n   177\t   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n   178\t   first clause of step 4.\n   179\t\n   180\tThe record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\n   181\tfact is never evidence of tenure.**\n   182\t\n   183\tA drain that ends in `:dao.stream/end` retires that cursor for subsequent\n   184\tpasses. A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/transport-error` ends the pass before\n   185\tstep 4.\n   186\t\n   187\t### Judging with incomplete evidence\n   188\t\n   189\tAbsence is evidence only over a window the judge observed. After a\n   190\t`:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is `unknown`,\n   191\tand a surviving older renewal is not inferred to be the newest. A lease\n   192\trecovered from a persisted ledger is `unknown`. Conditions other than\n   193\t`:silence` apply to an `unknown` lease unchanged.\n   194\t\n   195\t### Restart\n   196\t\n   197\tA grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\n   198\tresource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\n   199\tThe reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\n   200\t\n   201\tThis requires an inventory of possessed resources and a recoverable holder for\n   202\teach, both of which belong to the resource rather than to this vocabulary.\n   203\tA grantor with neither persists its ledger instead.\n   204\t\n   205\t## The holder\n   206\t\n   207\t- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n   208\t  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n   209\t  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n   210\t- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n   211\t  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n   212\t  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n   213\t- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n   214\t  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n   215\t  whether or not anything has been heard.\n   216\t- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n   217\t  it.\n   218\t- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n   219\t  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n   220\t  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n   221\t  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n   222\t\n   223\tFor cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\n   224\twhich it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n   225\tjudge's tenure start. The holder's observation-flight interval does not\n   226\textend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n   227\trequiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n   228\t\n   229\tThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n   230\tfrom the resource.\n   231\t\n   232\t## Composition duties\n   233\t\n   234\tA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n   235\t\n   236\t- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n   237\t  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n   238\t- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n   239\t- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n   240\t- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n   241\t  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n   242\t  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n   243\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   244\t  at assembly.\n   245\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n   246\t- **Over a durable ledger, a reclaim that only reports readiness.** The\n   247\t  revocation is the transaction that records `:lapsed` and its accompanying\n   248\t  facts; its commit is the linearization point. No state claiming a\n   249\t  successful revocation leaves the grantor's boundary before that commit. A\n   250\t  grantor stream that does not answer `:dao.stream/ok` leaves the lease\n   251\t  `pending` in the judge and the recorded state unchanged. A judge rebuilt\n   252\t  at restart drains its proposal media from their oldest anchor, so a\n   253\t  proposal drained but left unanswered before the restart is judged again.\n   254\t- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n   255\t  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n   256\t- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n   257\t  refused at assembly otherwise.\n   258\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   259\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   260\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n   261\t  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n   262\t\n   263\t## Sizing\n   264\t\n   265\tStated as relations; the values belong to compositions.\n   266\t\n   267\t- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.\n   268\t- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and the rate skew between\n   269\t  its tick stream and the holder's.\n   270\t- A medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag — cadence plus drain time.\n   271\t- A cap below the duration is permitted, and tenure then ends at the cap.\n   272\t- The holder's renewal interval plus one period of its tick stream is\n   273\t  strictly below half the granted duration: a renewal lands at the first\n   274\t  reading at or past the interval, up to one period late, so the\n   275\t  before-half guarantee is bought against interval plus period.\n   276\t\n   277\t## Limits\n   278\t\n   279\t- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\n   280\t  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n   281\t  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\n   282\t  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n   283\t  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\n   284\t- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n   285\t- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\n   286\t  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\n   287\t  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\n   288\t- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\n   289\t  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n   290\t\n   291\t## Carriage\n   292\t\n   293\tProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n   294\tpayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n   295\tis delivery, not a second authoring.\n   296\t\n   297\t`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n   298\tremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n   299\tordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\n   300\t`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\n   301\treads the grantor's stream through a served handle is observing the grantor's\n   302\trecord, not receiving carriage. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing\n   303\treclaim on the wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n   304\t\n   305\t## Out of scope\n   306\t\n   307\t- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n   308\t- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n   309\t- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\n   310\t  contract gates nothing, and the domain decides. `dao.stream.remote.md`\n   311\t  names two subjects: a stream entry served for a remote party, and a relay\n   312\t  pair on a meeting peer.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream WebSocket Transport\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implemented (`dao.stream.ws` and its host adapters), subordinate\n     4\tto `dao.stream.md`. That document is the\n     5\tcontract; this one specifies the WebSocket transport's implementation of it.\n     6\tWhere the two disagree, the contract wins.\n     7\t\n     8\t## What the Descriptor Names\n     9\t\n    10\tA WebSocket descriptor names a **stream held by the accepting peer**, never\n    11\ta connection. The stream exists on the peer that holds it — created there,\n    12\tretained there, closed there by its owner — and it exists whether or not\n    13\tany dialer is currently connected. A connection is the mechanics of one\n    14\tattachment: connecting joins the stream, disconnecting detaches from it, and\n    15\tneither event creates, closes, or destroys anything. That is what makes\n    16\t`attach!` honest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every\n    17\ttime, because the stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the peer that\n    18\tholds the stream, not to the wire.\n    19\t\n    20\tWhat this transport offers a dialer on its own is a **copy path**: values\n    21\tforwarded from a held source into each accepted connection and deposited on\n    22\tthe dialer's medium with that medium's own positions. Attachment to the\n    23\toriginal stream, with the source's own cursors and outcomes, is\n    24\t[`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md), which uses an attachment\n    25\tof this transport as a channel and owns no stream either. The transport\n    26\towns no stream — it serves streams the host composed, which is exactly the\n    27\tcontract's rule that descriptors resolve against \"the streams a network\n    28\tendpoint serves because serving them is what it is.\" An acceptor may also\n    29\tdial: which end dialed is an establishment fact, not a role.\n    30\t\n    31\tA peer that wants per-conversation state does not bend attachment into\n    32\tcreation: it `create!`s a fresh stream for the conversation and offers that\n    33\tstream's descriptor.\n    34\t\n    35\t## Surfaces\n    36\t\n    37\tA WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable**, plus `descriptor` as every\n    38\thandle. It has no reader surface: a socket retains nothing, so it cannot make\n    39\tthe reader surface's promise — a positioned, append-only, retained sequence\n    40\tthat cursors can observe and re-observe. `cursor` and `next` do not exist on it.\n    41\t\n    42\tBoth a dialer-side handle and an acceptor-side accepted-connection handle answer\n    43\t`descriptor` with reachability for the endpoint through which they attached\n    44\tand with the served stream's transport-independent `:dao.stream/identity`.\n    45\tTwo endpoints may therefore produce different descriptors for the same served\n    46\tstream, while every such result carries the same identity.\n    47\t`:dao.stream/attachment` distinguishes connections; neither the descriptor nor\n    48\tthe logical-stream identity does.\n    49\t\n    50\tThe writer surface is on the connection's **ordered outbound path** — the\n    51\tsequence of values accepted for sending on this attachment — never on the\n    52\tserved logical stream. `:dao.stream/ok` places the value in the outbound path;\n    53\twhat becomes of it at the far end is reported there, not here (contract,\n    54\tWriting).\n    55\t\n    56\tOutcomes this transport produces, and why it excludes the rest:\n    57\t\n    58\t| Operation    | Produces                                       | Excluded, and why                                                                             |\n    59\t|--------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n    60\t| `create!`    | —                                              | Absent: the transport owns no stream. Dynamic dispatch for this type returns `not-found`.     |\n    61\t| `attach!`    | `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, `transport-error`  | `transport-error` covers only failures decidable here and now — no socket API on this host, a constructor that fails, local resources exhausted. `not-found`, and the reachability failures that need a remote answer, are displaced to the deposit medium. |\n    62\t| `descriptor` | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |\n    63\t| `append!`    | `ok`, `full`, `invalid-value`, `closed`, `transport-error` | — (the contract's whole set)                                                      |\n    64\t| `close!`     | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |\n    65\t| `cursor`, `next` | —                                          | No reader surface, per above.                                                                 |\n    66\t\n    67\t## The Duplex Model\n    68\t\n    69\tA WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the\n    70\tdesign keeps them separate:\n    71\t\n    72\t- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and\n    73\t  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.\n    74\t- **Inbound**: wire framing and protocol validation first either produce a\n    75\t  valid transport event or perform protocol teardown. The host's adapter then\n    76\t  deposits every produced event — payloads and connection lifecycle events\n    77\t  alike, judging none semantically — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this\n    78\t  boundary. The deposit medium is an ordinary DaoStream stream the host\n    79\t  composed; this transport defines no separate event mechanism and no bus of\n    80\t  its own. (In the v2 migration slice that stream is a ring buffer, under a\n    81\t  time-boxed exception recorded in ADR 0003; the adapter cannot tell what it\n    82\t  deposits into, so the later swap is composition-only.) From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,\n    83\t  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that\n    84\t  stream.\n    85\t\n    86\tOn a client boundary, the traffic deposit destination is wired when the host\n    87\tcomposes the boundary, before `attach!`; it is not an argument chosen by the\n    88\tattaching interpreter. A serving endpoint instead receives the fixed\n    89\tacceptance-handoff slots and boundary control medium defined under Serving;\n    90\tthe acknowledgement supplies each accepted attachment's traffic destination.\n    91\tThese are host-composed media with different retention duties: a traffic\n    92\tmedium records replayable events and may report gaps, while a handoff slot\n    93\tholds one capability offer until that specific offer is acknowledged. The\n    94\tcomposition also hands each reader its medium and cursor. Every interpreter takes its own\n    95\tperspective on the same deposited truth: one indexes the traffic, another\n    96\tcopies it to a file, a third ignores it. How arrivals are interpreted is the\n    97\tinterpreter's business, not the transport's configuration.\n    98\t\n    99\t**Attachment identity.** This transport distinguishes its attachments, so its\n   100\t`attach!` success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` as the contract\n   101\trequires: a descriptor names the served stream and cannot tell two attachments\n   102\tto it apart, and a handle is a host-local object that cannot be compared\n   103\tagainst data that crossed a codec.\n   104\t\n   105\tEvery event deposited for that attachment carries the same value under\n   106\t`:ws/attachment`. The key differs because the scopes differ: result keywords\n   107\tare the contract's and live under `:dao.stream/…`, while a deposited event is\n   108\ta stream element, where transport-owned keys live under the transport's\n   109\tnamespace. Correlation is by the value carried, never by the key's name.\n   110\t\n   111\tWithout that identity a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence with no\n   112\tdemultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and unattributable.\n   113\t\n   114\t**Server-minted attachment identity.** Before offering a server-side\n   115\taccepted-connection handle to the serving composition, the transport mints\n   116\tits attachment identity under the contract's server-minted-handle rule. The\n   117\tvalue is unique among attachments the boundary can distinguish and is not\n   118\treused during that boundary's lifetime.\n   119\t\n   120\tThe acceptance-offer event carries the value under `:ws/attachment`, and\n   121\tevery later deposited event for the connection carries the same value. The\n   122\toffer, its acknowledgement, the handle, and all later events therefore\n   123\tdenote one attachment. Client-side `attach!` continues to return the\n   124\tcorresponding client-local identity under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   125\t\n   126\t**Deposited events are envelopes**, so that payload and lifecycle are\n   127\tdistinguishable and both are attributable:\n   128\t\n   129\t```clojure\n   130\t{:ws/attachment <id>\n   131\t :ws/event      :ws/payload  ; or :ws/accepted :ws/opened :ws/closed\n   132\t                             ;    :ws/ended :ws/error :ws/not-found\n   133\t                             ;    :ws/transport-error\n   134\t :ws/value      <decoded>    ; payload events only\n   135\t :ws/handle     {:dao.stream/handle <handle>\n   136\t                 :dao.stream/surface #{:writer :closable}}} ; accepted offers only; host-local\n   137\t```\n   138\t\n   139\tThe event kinds divide into four groups. **Acceptance offer** —\n   140\t`:ws/accepted` is deposited once into a serving handoff slot after the\n   141\trequested stream resolves and a server-side attachment handle is\n   142\tconstructed. It carries that writer+closable handle and its declared surface\n   143\tunder `:ws/handle`.\n   144\t**Resolution** — `:ws/opened`, `:ws/not-found`, or `:ws/transport-error` says\n   145\thow a client attachment turned out; exactly one resolution event is deposited\n   146\tper attachment, and it is what a retry policy reads. **Lifecycle** —\n   147\t`:ws/closed` when the connection went away and `:ws/ended` when the served\n   148\tstream itself ended (see Ending a served stream). **Traffic** —\n   149\t`:ws/payload`, carrying `:ws/value`, and `:ws/error`. `:ws/error` is a diagnostic and is never terminal by itself. A survivable socket error may be followed by no lifecycle event; a protocol failure is followed by the terminal event produced when its required teardown completes.\n   150\t\n   151\tOnce an attachment identity exists, **every connection end deposits exactly\n   152\tone terminal lifecycle event at each endpoint**, whether the close was local,\n   153\tremote, or caused by failed deposit, protocol teardown, admission expiry, or\n   154\tendpoint stop. `:ws/ended` is reserved for a served-stream close; every other\n   155\tend is `:ws/closed`. For a code-4000 served-stream close, both the initiating\n   156\tserver endpoint and the observing client endpoint deposit `:ws/ended`. A\n   157\tresolution event, if any, is deposited first. Thus a\n   158\tdisclaimer is observed as `:ws/not-found` followed by `:ws/closed`, while a\n   159\tpre-resolution connection failure is `:ws/transport-error` followed by\n   160\t`:ws/closed`. Only the host close-completion callback deposits the terminal\n   161\tevent, guarded by attachment state so racing close/error callbacks cannot\n   162\tduplicate it.\n   163\t\n   164\tAn accepted offer is host-local and never crosses a serialization boundary.\n   165\tIt does not share the traffic medium's retention window; Serving defines its\n   166\tbounded handoff medium and acknowledgement.\n   167\t\n   168\t**The adapter is the sole host-socket subscriber, and the raw socket never\n   169\tescapes the handle.** On hosts with additive registration it uses\n   170\t`addEventListener` or `.on`; on Dart it owns the socket's single subscription.\n   171\tIn every case it fans transformed events into the composed deposit stream.\n   172\t“Additive” describes adding interpreters and cursors to that stream, never\n   173\tadding another listener to the raw socket.\n   174\t\n   175\tWrapping is not judging — the adapter never looks inside `:ws/value`.\n   176\tDeposited-event envelopes do not cross the wire: attachment correlation is\n   177\tadded locally because each side already knows which socket delivered a frame.\n   178\tWire frames have their own minimal envelope, specified under The Handshake and\n   179\tElements and Serialization, so protocol control cannot be confused with an\n   180\tapplication value.\n   181\t\n   182\t**Traffic granularity is composition data.** A client boundary may reuse one\n   183\tmedium across reconnects because it has one active attachment at a time. A\n   184\tserving endpoint uses one application-traffic medium per accepted attachment,\n   185\tsupplied with the acknowledgement; only endpoint/pre-accept control facts may\n   186\tshare its boundary control medium. This does not alter acceptance handoff:\n   187\tserver writer capabilities travel through bounded handoff slots, never through\n   188\tevictable traffic history.\n   189\t\n   190\t```clojure\n   191\t(def result (ws/attach! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws\n   192\t                         :ws/host \"example.org\"\n   193\t                         :ws/port 9090\n   194\t                         :ws/path \"/streams/telemetry\"}))\n   195\t\n   196\t(def sock (:dao.stream/handle result))\n   197\t(def me   (:dao.stream/attachment result))   ; keep it — nothing else identifies\n   198\t                                             ; this attachment on the medium\n   199\t\n   200\t;; write: (append! sock msg)\n   201\t;; read:  interpreters observe the medium the host boundary deposits into,\n   202\t;;        keeping events whose :ws/attachment is `me`\n   203\t```\n   204\t\n   205\tNo inbound-event inbox accumulates inside the transport: a hidden per-socket\n   206\tinbox would be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed,\n   207\tsized by nobody. Before acceptance acknowledgement, the endpoint retains only\n   208\tthe bounded pending connection associated with each configured handoff slot.\n   209\tAfter acknowledgement, retention of traffic lives exclusively in the\n   210\thost-composed traffic medium, composed and sized by the host.\n   211\t\n   212\tThere are no callback hooks — no `on-open!` / `on-message!` / `on-close!`\n   213\tsurface of the kind the host's own socket API offers. Every socket event\n   214\tbecomes data on the deposit stream — payload and lifecycle (opened, closed by peer, error)\n   215\talike, distinguishable by shape. A boundary deposits everything; whether\n   216\tanyone reads it is not the boundary's concern.\n   217\t\n   218\t### Serving\n   219\t\n   220\tServing is the mirror of attaching, and the deposit model is the same on both\n   221\tends: each accepted connection's inbound messages are deposited — enveloped\n   222\twith that connection's attachment identity, exactly as on the client — onto a\n   223\tstream the host composition wired.\n   224\t\n   225\t**Acceptance is a bounded, acknowledged stream handoff.** At endpoint\n   226\tconstruction, the host composition supplies a fixed, non-empty collection of\n   227\thandoff slots. Each slot contains:\n   228\t\n   229\t- a host-local offer medium with evict-oldest retention and capacity one,\n   230\t  written by the transport and read by the composition; and\n   231\t- a host-local acknowledgement medium with evict-oldest retention and\n   232\t  capacity one, written by the composition and polled by the endpoint driver.\n   233\t\n   234\tThe composition mints the offer cursor it will retain and the acknowledgement\n   235\tcursor `endpoint-step` will retain for every slot before starting the listener.\n   236\tNo connection can therefore deposit an offer before its reader position exists.\n   237\t\n   238\tThe transport has at most one outstanding attachment in a slot. After\n   239\tresolving the requested stream, it assigns a free slot, mints the attachment\n   240\tidentity and handle, retains the connection in the endpoint's bounded\n   241\tpending-accept state, and deposits:\n   242\t\n   243\t```clojure\n   244\t{:ws/attachment <id>\n   245\t :ws/event      :ws/accepted\n   246\t :ws/handle     {:dao.stream/handle <server-side-writer-handle>\n   247\t                 :dao.stream/surface #{:writer :closable}}}\n   248\t```\n   249\t\n   250\tinto that slot's offer medium. It does not send the wire `:ws/accept` frame\n   251\tor enable value delivery yet.\n   252\t\n   253\tThe serving composition polls the known offer slots. After it reads an offer\n   254\tand retains the handle in its own session state, it appends:\n   255\t\n   256\t```clojure\n   257\t{:ws/attachment <id>\n   258\t :ws/command    :ws/accept\n   259\t :ws/deposit    {:dao.stream/handle <writer-handle>\n   260\t                 :dao.stream/surface #{:writer}}\n   261\t :ws/admission  {:retention :evict-oldest\n   262\t                 :capacity <positive-integer>\n   263\t                 :value-domain :host-values}}\n   264\t```\n   265\t\n   266\tto that slot's acknowledgement medium. The composition creates this\n   267\tper-attachment traffic medium and mints its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor before\n   268\tdepositing the acknowledgement, retaining the reader and cursor in session\n   269\tstate. The transport validates the writer and admission declaration before\n   270\tenabling delivery. A wrong-identity acknowledgement is stale and ignored\n   271\twithout changing the outstanding slot. A matching acknowledgement with a\n   272\tmalformed writer, surface, or admission declaration is rejected:\n   273\t`endpoint-step` releases the slot, closes the pending connection before\n   274\tacceptance, and its close-completion deposits `:ws/closed` on the control\n   275\tmedium.\n   276\t\n   277\tEvery event before acceptance is enabled — including decode diagnostics,\n   278\tresolution, and the terminal event for pre-accept teardown — lands in the\n   279\tboundary control medium. Every payload, diagnostic, and terminal event after\n   280\tacceptance is enabled lands in that attachment's acknowledged traffic medium.\n   281\tThe serving composition reads both: the control medium retires a pending\n   282\toffer/session, while the per-attachment medium retires an established session.\n   283\t\n   284\tThe transport exports an explicit\n   285\t`endpoint-step` operation. The serving composition's single driver calls it\n   286\twith endpoint state and an explicit `now`; the step accepts only an\n   287\tacknowledgement whose identity matches the slot's outstanding offer, sends the\n   288\twire `:ws/accept` frame, enables value delivery, and releases the slot for\n   289\treuse. It never schedules itself. Admission expiry is a constructor policy\n   290\texpressed in the same clock domain as `now`, and `nil` means no expiry. Stale\n   291\tacknowledgements are ignored; attachment identities are not reused during the\n   292\tboundary lifetime.\n   293\t\n   294\tAn outstanding accepted offer cannot be evicted: it is the slot's sole\n   295\tvalue, and the transport does not append another offer to that slot before\n   296\tacknowledgement. Reuse may later evict the acknowledged offer, but by then\n   297\tthe composition has both read the event and retained its handle. A reader\n   298\tlagging before acknowledgement prevents slot reuse rather than losing the\n   299\tcapability. Thus eviction of an unacknowledged acceptance event is\n   300\timpossible by construction.\n   301\t\n   302\tThe handoff remains bounded. When no slot is free, the transport admits no\n   303\tadditional connection; it closes the new socket before logical acceptance,\n   304\tand the client resolves it as `:ws/transport-error`. It neither overwrites\n   305\tan outstanding offer nor grows an unbounded pending queue.\n   306\t\n   307\tOwnership remains explicit in every failure case:\n   308\t\n   309\t- if the offer deposit is non-`ok`, the endpoint closes the pending\n   310\t  connection;\n   311\t- before acknowledgement, the endpoint retains the handle and can close it\n   312\t  on peer loss, endpoint stop, or the configured admission expiry; teardown\n   313\t  releases the slot, emits the one applicable terminal lifecycle event, and\n   314\t  makes any later acknowledgement stale;\n   315\t- after reading the offer, the composition also holds the handle and closes\n   316\t  it if acknowledgement cannot be deposited;\n   317\t- after acknowledgement, the composition owns the session handle, while the\n   318\t  endpoint continues to own its host connection for endpoint-wide stop;\n   319\t- any non-`ok` traffic deposit after acceptance tears down the connection\n   320\t  under Deposit Admission.\n   321\t\n   322\tNo callback reaches the composition. Offer and acknowledgement are ordinary\n   323\tstream values, and the composition-owned driver invokes `endpoint-step`\n   324\twithout waiting or installing a waiter.\n   325\t\n   326\t**That stream is not the served stream.** A client's `append!` is a request to\n   327\tthe server, never a broadcast: what a client writes lands in a medium only the\n   328\tserving host's own interpreters read. Nothing a client sends reaches the other\n   329\tattachments by default.\n   330\t\n   331\tThe alternative — depositing a client's messages straight into the served\n   332\tstream — would make every client a writer to the stream every other client is\n   333\treading, by nothing more than holding a handle. A composition that wants that\n   334\twrites it explicitly: an interpreter reads the request medium, decides what\n   335\tqualifies, and appends to the served stream. Then the admission rule is a\n   336\tpiece of code someone wrote, in one place, rather than a consequence of the\n   337\ttransport's shape.\n   338\t\n   339\t### Ending a served stream\n   340\t\n   341\tClosing a served stream is its owner's `close!`, and connected clients have to\n   342\tbe able to tell that from a connection that merely dropped. The two call for\n   343\topposite responses — a dropped connection means reattach, an ended stream\n   344\tmeans stop — and a client has no reader surface, so the answer can only reach\n   345\tit as a deposited event.\n   346\t\n   347\tThe closing frame carries it. When the serving host closes a stream it closes\n   348\teach attached connection **with a distinguishing close code**, rather than\n   349\tdropping the socket. The client's adapter reads that code and deposits\n   350\t`:ws/ended` instead of `:ws/closed`. (The exact code is `4000`, mapping to\n   351\t`:ws/ended`, while `4002` is protocol-error and `4004` is the authoritative\n   352\tnot-found disclaimer; see Elements and Serialization.)\n   353\t\n   354\tWhat separates the two cases is not the closing but whether anything was\n   355\theard. A connection lost to a network fault delivers no closing frame at all,\n   356\tand the host reports that distinctly — the client learns that nobody told it\n   357\tanything, and deposits `:ws/closed`.\n   358\t\n   359\tThree outcomes follow, and the third is the honest limit:\n   360\t\n   361\t- **A closing frame arrived** — the stream ended. Stop; there is nothing to\n   362\t  reattach to.\n   363\t- **Nothing arrived** — reattach. If the stream is in fact gone, the handshake\n   364\t  answers with the authoritative disclaimer and the client learns on that\n   365\t  attempt instead of this one. A frame lost in transit costs one wasted\n   366\t  reattach, never a wrong conclusion the client cannot escape.\n   367\t- **The server cannot be reached at all** — unknowable. Nothing can distinguish\n   368\t  a crashed peer from an unreachable one, so a client keeps retrying, which is\n   369\t  correct: the stream may still be there. Bounding those retries is the\n   370\t  consumer's policy, not this transport's.\n   371\t\n   372\tThe close code is the fast path for the common case — a healthy server ending a\n   373\tstream deliberately. The handshake's disclaimer is what makes every other case\n   374\tresolve as soon as a server is reachable. Neither is sufficient alone.\n   375\t\n   376\t### Deposit Admission\n   377\t\n   378\tThe adapter runs in a callback context: it cannot wait, cannot park, and\n   379\tcannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the\n   380\tdeposit destination must be one that always admits, and this is enforced\n   381\twhere it can be — at assembly, against the destination's declared nature:\n   382\t\n   383\tAcceptance handoff media obey the same declared-admission rule: both media\n   384\tin every slot declare host-local reference admission, evict-oldest retention,\n   385\tcapacity one, ability to carry their respective envelope (including the live\n   386\thandle in an offer), and a lifetime longer than the endpoint. Their additional\n   387\tone-outstanding-offer discipline — not a different retention mode — is what\n   388\tprevents capability loss.\n   389\t\n   390\tThe acknowledgement's per-attachment traffic medium obeys the same rule and\n   391\tmust outlive that attachment. Supplying it in the acknowledgement makes its\n   392\tcursor-before-delivery ordering explicit and isolates one attachment's payload\n   393\tretention pressure from every other attachment.\n   394\t\n   395\tThe server boundary control medium also declares evict-oldest retention,\n   396\tcapacity, `:portable-values`, and a lifetime longer than the endpoint. Its\n   397\tcursor is minted before listener start, as are the handoff cursors.\n   398\t\n   399\t- The destination's assembly declaration contains both **retention** and\n   400\t  **value domain**. Retention is evict-oldest with a host-declared capacity.\n   401\t  The value domain is either `:host-values`, which may retain host references,\n   402\t  or `:portable-values`, which admits exactly the boundary codec's structural\n   403\t  domain. A WebSocket traffic medium may use either; a handoff offer medium\n   404\t  must use `:host-values` because it carries a live handle. It never answers\n   405\t  `:dao.stream/full`; under\n   406\t  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s to lagging cursors — the one\n   407\t  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an\n   408\t  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the\n   409\t  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed\n   410\t  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting\n   411\t  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,\n   412\t  like any storage failure.)\n   413\t- The destination must also be able to **carry every envelope this transport\n   414\t  deposits** — attachment identifiers, lifecycle events, and every value the\n   415\t  codec admits. A destination that evicts oldest but answers\n   416\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-value` on some envelope is as unwired as a reject-mode\n   417\t  one; retention mode alone is not admission.\n   418\t- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused\n   419\t  when the boundary is composed. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before\n   420\t  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.\n   421\t- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit\n   422\t  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is\n   423\t  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination\n   424\t  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;\n   425\t  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary\n   426\t  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.\n   427\t  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the\n   428\t  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and\n   429\t  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary\n   430\t  `attach!`.\n   431\t\n   432\tSizing the window is choosing how much cursor lag to tolerate before gaps\n   433\tappear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Whether\n   434\tthat window can be resized after the fact is the destination transport's\n   435\tdeclared nature, not this transport's business. An adaptive policy — grow on\n   436\trising gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own\n   437\ttelemetry, not a transport behavior.\n   438\t\n   439\tThe accounting is then honest, though not complete: declared retention\n   440\tpressure surfaces as `gap`s to cursors that span the eviction, and a dead\n   441\tdestination tears the\n   442\tattachment down. But a deposit that fails is a deposit that did not land, and\n   443\tneither can the teardown event land in the destination that just refused it —\n   444\tso in that one case the local failure is observable only through the deposit\n   445\tresult the adapter holds and through subsequent handle operations, and the\n   446\tpeer observes it only if teardown reaches the wire.\n   447\t\n   448\t## Envelope\n   449\t\n   450\tTransport-owned keys are qualified under `:ws/…`; the logical-stream identity\n   451\tkey is contract-owned. The exact key set is settled by the descriptor gate;\n   452\tthese properties constrain it:\n   453\t\n   454\t- The descriptor is self-contained and exists to cross the network: it\n   455\t  carries what any host needs to reach the served stream — advertised host,\n   456\t  advertised port, canonical path, perhaps TLS parameters — plus the distinct\n   457\t  `:dao.stream/identity`. Endpoint address alone does not name a stream.\n   458\t- An endpoint constructor receives bind host/port and advertised host/port as\n   459\t  separate composition data. Bind defaults to `127.0.0.1`. An omitted\n   460\t  advertised host defaults to a concrete bind host, but a wildcard bind\n   461\t  requires an explicit advertised host. An omitted advertised port defaults to\n   462\t  the actual bound port, including the assigned port after a bind to zero.\n   463\t  Descriptors returned by accepted handles use the advertised values.\n   464\t- The descriptor carries reachability and logical-stream identity only. Where inbound traffic lands is the\n   465\t  host boundary's composition (see The Duplex Model) and appears in no\n   466\t  envelope and no argument of `attach!`.\n   467\t\n   468\t## The Handshake\n   469\t\n   470\tAttaching presents the descriptor's canonical lookup path, and the endpoint\n   471\teither accepts it or **authoritatively disclaims** it. Both halves are wire protocol\n   472\towned by this transport, not by any composition: a client must recognize the\n   473\tdisclaimer to deposit `:ws/not-found` rather than a generic failure, so a\n   474\tcomposition inventing its own would not interoperate with any client. What a\n   475\tcomposition owns is only which identities it serves — its resolution table —\n   476\tand never the vocabulary of asking and disclaiming.\n   477\t\n   478\tAttaching presents `:ws/path` as the WebSocket HTTP request-target path.\n   479\t`:ws/path` is a canonical, non-empty absolute path beginning with `/`. URI dot\n   480\tsegments are removed; percent-encoded unreserved octets are decoded; remaining\n   481\tpercent hex digits are upper-case; an empty URI path becomes `/`; and encoded\n   482\tslashes remain encoded. Repeated slashes and a trailing slash are significant.\n   483\tQuery and fragment components are ignored for lookup and are absent from the\n   484\tdescriptor. Resolution is an exact string lookup on this canonical form.\n   485\t\n   486\tSubprotocol selection is explicit on both ends, because the wire codec is a\n   487\tcomposition choice carried as a **codec profile**: the subprotocol name it\n   488\tnegotiates, its frame kind (`:text` or `:binary`), the portable-value\n   489\tpredicate for its domain, and its encoder and decoder. Two profiles exist —\n   490\t`dao.stream.transit-json` (text frames) and `dao.stream.cbor` (binary\n   491\tframes) — and both run the same state machine, envelopes, and lifecycle. A\n   492\tclient offers exactly the subprotocol of its selected profile; an endpoint\n   493\tserves exactly the profiles it was composed with, concurrently and through\n   494\tthe same served paths. The endpoint negotiates deterministically: a named\n   495\toffer it does not serve is refused before the upgrade completes, never\n   496\tsilently downgraded to a codec the client did not select, and an absent\n   497\tname is the `dao.stream.transit-json` default for sockets that negotiated\n   498\tnothing.\n   499\t\n   500\tAfter upgrade, the first WebSocket message sent by the server is exactly one\n   501\tframe in the negotiated codec — one Transit-JSON text frame or one CBOR\n   502\tbinary frame:\n   503\t\n   504\t```clojure\n   505\t{:ws/frame :ws/accept}\n   506\t```\n   507\t\n   508\tor:\n   509\t\n   510\t```clojure\n   511\t{:ws/frame :ws/disclaim}\n   512\t```\n   513\t\n   514\t`:ws/accept` authoritatively accepts the presented path and causes the\n   515\tclient boundary to deposit `:ws/opened`. `:ws/disclaim` authoritatively\n   516\tdisclaims it and causes the client boundary to deposit `:ws/not-found`; the\n   517\tserver then closes with code `4004` and reason `dao.stream/not-found`. The\n   518\tclient does not deposit `:ws/opened` merely because the HTTP upgrade\n   519\tcompleted.\n   520\t\n   521\tPath resolution and `:ws/disclaim` happen in the upgrade callback. A\n   522\tnot-found connection never occupies an acceptance slot and does not wait for\n   523\t`endpoint-step`; slot-exhaustion teardown is likewise immediate and bounded by\n   524\tthe host's upgrade queue.\n   525\t\n   526\tNo value frame may be sent before `:ws/accept`. A frame of any other shape in\n   527\tthe first position is a protocol failure. If the connection closes before\n   528\teither `:ws/accept` or `:ws/disclaim` is received, the client deposits\n   529\t`:ws/transport-error` as that attachment's resolution.\n   530\t\n   531\t## Operations\n   532\t\n   533\t- There is no `create!`. The transport owns no stream, so it creates none;\n   534\t  a host serves a stream it composed elsewhere, and starting an endpoint is\n   535\t  a host composition step, not a DaoStream operation. A creation\n   536\t  specification naming this transport returns `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   537\t- `attach!` begins connecting and returns without waiting, per the\n   538\t  contract's rule that no operation waits. It answers what is decidable\n   539\t  locally, at the moment it is called: `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` for\n   540\t  a descriptor it cannot read, `:dao.stream/transport-error` for a handler\n   541\t  that fails here and now — no socket API on this host, a constructor that\n   542\t  fails, local resources exhausted — and otherwise `:dao.stream/ok` with a\n   543\t  handle on an attachment still being established. It never returns an\n   544\t  answer it has not heard. This is not a concession to convenience: on a host with no\n   545\t  blocking IO the endpoint's answer cannot arrive before `attach!` returns,\n   546\t  and an `attach!` that waited for it would have to hand back a promise —\n   547\t  breaking the result convention on every host that cannot block, and giving\n   548\t  the same transport different outcome sets per host.\n   549\t- **How the connection resolved arrives as data**, on the same host-composed\n   550\t  medium as every other socket event, correlated by attachment identity.\n   551\t  The distinction survives intact, only its channel moves. These are\n   552\t  deposited elements, not outcome maps, so they carry the transport's own\n   553\t  vocabulary, and exactly one resolution event is deposited per attachment:\n   554\t  `:ws/opened` when the endpoint accepted the stream, and otherwise one of the\n   555\t  two failures. `:ws/not-found` is the authoritative answer that what the\n   556\t  descriptor names does not exist — the endpoint responded and disclaimed\n   557\t  the stream — and is not retried automatically (though a server may serve\n   558\t  that identity later; retrying on external evidence is interpreter\n   559\t  policy), while `:ws/transport-error` covers transient\n   560\t  reachability and connection failures — DNS, timeout, routing, refusal —\n   561\t  and retrying it may succeed on its own. A caller's retry policy is as\n   562\t  deterministic as before; it reads the answer from the medium instead of\n   563\t  from a return value.\n   564\t- `append!` returns `:dao.stream/full` when the send buffer cannot accept\n   565\t  the value now — **transient**: the network drains the buffer on its own,\n   566\t  and a later append succeeds. An attachment is establishing until the client\n   567\t  has **received** `:ws/accept` (or, server-side, until `endpoint-step` has sent\n   568\t  it and enabled delivery); throughout that interval `append!` returns `full`\n   569\t  and sends nothing.\n   570\t  `:dao.stream/closed` once the connection is down, which includes an\n   571\t  attachment whose connection never succeeded. A value that cannot be\n   572\t  encoded for the wire is `:dao.stream/invalid-value`. A local send that\n   573\t  fails for neither reason is `:dao.stream/transport-error`. Operating the\n   574\t  handle therefore tells a caller **that** the attachment is not writable,\n   575\t  needing no reader at all — but never why: an authoritative `not-found` and\n   576\t  a transient failure both reduce to `closed` here, and the distinction\n   577\t  survives only as the correlated **resolution** event on the deposit medium\n   578\t  — never as a lifecycle event, which is precisely the class that does not\n   579\t  carry it.\n   580\t- `close!` on an attached handle means **disconnect from the server**: the\n   581\t  handle is on the attachment, so closing it ends the connection,\n   582\t  idempotently, and this handle's subsequent `append!` returns\n   583\t  `:dao.stream/closed`. The served stream is untouched — closing *it*\n   584\t  belongs to its server-side owner. Disconnection is not final: the\n   585\t  descriptor remains valid and `attach!` rejoins the same stream. The\n   586\t  close-completion callback at each endpoint deposits the departure into its\n   587\t  host-composed event medium, correlated by attachment identity. If releasing\n   588\t  the host socket fails after the local state transition, the local traffic\n   589\t  medium first receives\n   590\t  `{:ws/attachment <id> :ws/event :ws/error :ws/reason :ws/close-failure}`;\n   591\t  the later close-completion still contributes the single terminal lifecycle\n   592\t  event. A diagnostic deposit that itself fails follows Deposit Admission.\n   593\t- Connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited: the served stream\n   594\t  persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` with the same\n   595\t  descriptor rejoins the same stream. What a client must not confuse with it\n   596\t  is the served stream *ending*, which is a different fact reaching it by a\n   597\t  different route (see Ending a served stream).\n   598\t\n   599\t## Elements and Serialization\n   600\t\n   601\tThe wire is a serialization boundary. Every element must survive the codec\n   602\tstructurally unchanged; a handle can never travel — its portable\n   603\tdescriptor can.\n   604\t\n   605\tThe wire codec is the connection's codec profile (see The Handshake):\n   606\t**Transit JSON encoded as one UTF-8 WebSocket text message per value**, or\n   607\t**canonical CBOR encoded as one WebSocket binary message per value**. Every\n   608\tapplication value is framed as:\n   609\t\n   610\t```clojure\n   611\t{:ws/frame :ws/value\n   612\t :ws/value <value>}\n   613\t```\n   614\t\n   615\tThe portable value domain is `nil`, booleans, strings, qualified or\n   616\tunqualified keywords and symbols, safe integers in\n   617\t`[-9007199254740991, 9007199254740991]`, finite doubles, vectors, lists,\n   618\tsets, and maps recursively composed from that domain. The Transit profile is\n   619\texactly that domain — no custom handlers and no metadata participate. The\n   620\tCBOR profile is that domain widened with metadata — recursively portable\n   621\tmetadata on collections and symbols, reader positions included — with host\n   622\tbyte payloads (`byte[]` / `Uint8Array` / `Uint8List`) riding as CBOR byte\n   623\tstrings (major type 2), the carrier `dao.jing.stream`'s boundary adapter\n   624\tputs Jing's canonical bytes on, and it\n   625\tkeeps lists and vectors distinct: identifiers ride CBOR tag 39, sets ride\n   626\ttag 258, and metadata and list-hood ride tag 27 frames (`clojure/with-meta`\n   627\tand `dao.stream/list`). A sender unable to encode a value in its profile's\n   628\tdomain returns `:dao.stream/invalid-value` and sends nothing. Across hosts,\n   629\tnumeric structural equality is by mathematical value within this domain: an\n   630\tintegral finite double and the equal safe integer are equivalent even if a\n   631\thost codec materializes different numeric classes.\n   632\t\n   633\tThe CBOR wire is **canonical CBOR only, enforced on receive**: a receiver\n   634\tdecodes, re-encodes, and byte-compares, so an indefinite length, a\n   635\tnon-minimal integer, a non-shortest float, duplicate map keys, or map keys\n   636\toutside canonical bytewise order are protocol failures, not silently\n   637\tnormalized input. The price is roughly one extra encode per inbound\n   638\tmessage; the return is a byte-exact wire across all three hosts and a\n   639\tduplicate-key guard no host codec can skip.\n   640\t\n   641\tAll protocol keywords use exactly one namespace separator — for example\n   642\t`:ws/frame`, never a multi-slash spelling such as `:ws/wire/frame` — so the\n   643\tvocabulary reads on clj, cljs, and cljd.\n   644\t\n   645\tA message of the profile's wrong frame kind, a text message that is not\n   646\tvalid Transit JSON, a binary payload that is not canonical CBOR inside the\n   647\tprofile's portable domain, an unknown `:ws/frame` value, a missing required\n   648\tkey, a value outside the portable domain, or a `:ws/value` frame received\n   649\tbefore `:ws/accept` has been sent is\n   650\ta protocol failure. Once an attachment identity exists, the\n   651\treceiver first deposits:\n   652\t\n   653\t```clojure\n   654\t{:ws/attachment <id>\n   655\t :ws/event      :ws/error\n   656\t :ws/reason     :ws/decode-failure}\n   657\t```\n   658\t\n   659\tand then closes the connection with code `4002` and reason\n   660\t`dao.stream/protocol-error`. The malformed input and host error object are\n   661\tnot deposited. If the error deposit itself fails, the ordinary\n   662\tdeposit-admission teardown rule applies.\n   663\t\n   664\tClose code `4000`, reason `dao.stream/ended`, maps to `:ws/ended`. Code `4004` is the authoritative not-found close following the disclaimer frame. Code `4002` is a protocol-error teardown. For an established attachment, completion of a 4002 teardown deposits exactly one `:ws/closed` at both the endpoint that initiated the close and the peer that observed it. The detecting endpoint therefore deposits `:ws/error` first and `:ws/closed` when the connection terminates. Before client resolution, closure before `:ws/accept` or `:ws/disclaim` deposits `:ws/transport-error` and then the single `:ws/closed`; after `:ws/disclaim`, it deposits `:ws/not-found` and then `:ws/closed`. Every other peer close or connection loss maps to `:ws/closed` under the exactly-once lifecycle rule.\n   665\t\n   666\t## Deferred\n   667\t\n   668\t- The establishment-cancel gap on the JVM edge: `close!` before the socket\n   669\t  exists records a `:close-request` and returns, leaving the pending\n   670\t  `buildAsync` future untouched, and the stored request is applied only by\n   671\t  an `onOpen` that a peer stalled mid-handshake never triggers — while the\n   672\t  `HttpClient` carries no connect or request timeout, so a peer that\n   673\t  accepts TCP and stalls the upgrade holds the client-side connection for\n   674\t  the process lifetime. Cancelling the future would run the observer\n   675\t  `connect!` already registers — the terminal deposit is already guarded —\n   676\t  but `CompletableFuture` cancellation does not propagate to the stage\n   677\t  producing the socket, so a repair must capture the future, mark the\n   678\t  connection terminal under the submission monitor, and issue the cancel\n   679\t  only after releasing it (a completed future's inline observer would\n   680\t  deposit under the lock, the hazard the send-seam repair removed); the\n   681\t  prior-close check and the socket install in `onOpen` must become one\n   682\t  transition under that same monitor, with a rejected socket's abort\n   683\t  performed outside it; and `send!`'s no-socket branch must then consult\n   684\t  the failure flag rather than answering `full` unconditionally, or a late\n   685\t  append is told to retry against a socket that will never exist. The\n   686\t  shipped `before-open-send-answers-full` test builds its connection with\n   687\t  no watched future, so a repair that cancels on close must give it one.\n   688\t  Even repaired, the residual is unbounded for a stalled peer and\n   689\t  caller-owned: a late `onOpen` can be aborted, but the JDK offers no\n   690\t  handle to bound a peer that never completes the handshake. `yin.repl`\n   691\t  is this gap's first consumer and `dao.jing.remote` (deprecated by\n   692\t  `dao.stream.remote.md`) its second, and neither can close it from where\n   693\t  it stands.\n   694\t- Exact envelope key set (tracks the contract's descriptor TBD).\n   695\t- Liveness: whether an idle connection is probed with the protocol's own\n   696\t  ping/pong frames or with ordinary deposited values, how often, how many\n   697\t  unanswered probes end a connection, and — if the probe is not a protocol\n   698\t  frame — which component is obliged to answer it. For a connection used as\n   699\t  a `dao.stream.remote.md` channel, a `descriptor` request is the probe and\n   700\t  the mirror step answers it; cadence stays the composition's.\n   701\t- Resumption protocol: stream identity is stable across reconnects, so\n   702\t  resumption is possible by design; what history a rejoining dialer receives\n   703\t  (and how it states where it left off) is not yet specified for the copy\n   704\t  path. Through `dao.stream.remote.md` a kept cursor is the whole answer.\n   705\t- Backpressure protocols above the stream (a reader publishing its progress\n   706\t  as data is interpretation, outside both this transport and the contract).\n   707\t  Needing nothing from this transport does not mean needing no specification:\n   708\t  a pause vocabulary both ends must recognize is interoperable wire content,\n   709\t  and it requires a durable home before it is built. `dao.lease.md` is the\n   710\t  intended home for its semantics and does not yet claim them; the wire form\n   711\t  is owed by neither that document nor this one until it does. The v2\n   712\t  migration plan has deferred flow control out of its slice for the same\n   713\t  reason, so nothing is being built against an unsettled vocabulary.\n   714\t- Some host socket APIs expose no reliable outbound high-water signal after\n   715\t  establishment. On such a host `append!` may accept into the host's opaque\n   716\t  buffer and the manifest excludes transient `full` for that state; this\n   717\t  bounded-observability asymmetry is explicit rather than an invented signal.\n   718\t- Relationship to the RPC layers built over streams: `dao.stream.remote.md`\n   719\t  section 8 retires `dao.stream.rpc.ws` and `dao.stream.serving` and makes\n   720\t  request and response a convention over remote streams.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
