{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '76,270p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -A65 -B5 'Deposit Admission' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md\"","aggregated_output":"315-- after reading the offer, the composition also holds the handle and closes\n316-  it if acknowledgement cannot be deposited;\n317-- after acknowledgement, the composition owns the session handle, while the\n318-  endpoint continues to own its host connection for endpoint-wide stop;\n319-- any non-`ok` traffic deposit after acceptance tears down the connection\n320:  under Deposit Admission.\n321-\n322-No callback reaches the composition. Offer and acknowledgement are ordinary\n323-stream values, and the composition-owned driver invokes `endpoint-step`\n324-without waiting or installing a waiter.\n325-\n326-**That stream is not the served stream.** A client's `append!` is a request to\n327-the server, never a broadcast: what a client writes lands in a medium only the\n328-serving host's own interpreters read. Nothing a client sends reaches the other\n329-attachments by default.\n330-\n331-The alternative — depositing a client's messages straight into the served\n332-stream — would make every client a writer to the stream every other client is\n333-reading, by nothing more than holding a handle. A composition that wants that\n334-writes it explicitly: an interpreter reads the request medium, decides what\n335-qualifies, and appends to the served stream. Then the admission rule is a\n336-piece of code someone wrote, in one place, rather than a consequence of the\n337-transport's shape.\n338-\n339-### Ending a served stream\n340-\n341-Closing a served stream is its owner's `close!`, and connected clients have to\n342-be able to tell that from a connection that merely dropped. The two call for\n343-opposite responses — a dropped connection means reattach, an ended stream\n344-means stop — and a client has no reader surface, so the answer can only reach\n345-it as a deposited event.\n346-\n347-The closing frame carries it. When the serving host closes a stream it closes\n348-each attached connection **with a distinguishing close code**, rather than\n349-dropping the socket. The client's adapter reads that code and deposits\n350-`:ws/ended` instead of `:ws/closed`. (The exact code is `4000`, mapping to\n351-`:ws/ended`, while `4002` is protocol-error and `4004` is the authoritative\n352-not-found disclaimer; see Elements and Serialization.)\n353-\n354-What separates the two cases is not the closing but whether anything was\n355-heard. A connection lost to a network fault delivers no closing frame at all,\n356-and the host reports that distinctly — the client learns that nobody told it\n357-anything, and deposits `:ws/closed`.\n358-\n359-Three outcomes follow, and the third is the honest limit:\n360-\n361-- **A closing frame arrived** — the stream ended. Stop; there is nothing to\n362-  reattach to.\n363-- **Nothing arrived** — reattach. If the stream is in fact gone, the handshake\n364-  answers with the authoritative disclaimer and the client learns on that\n365-  attempt instead of this one. A frame lost in transit costs one wasted\n366-  reattach, never a wrong conclusion the client cannot escape.\n367-- **The server cannot be reached at all** — unknowable. Nothing can distinguish\n368-  a crashed peer from an unreachable one, so a client keeps retrying, which is\n369-  correct: the stream may still be there. Bounding those retries is the\n370-  consumer's policy, not this transport's.\n371-\n372-The close code is the fast path for the common case — a healthy server ending a\n373-stream deliberately. The handshake's disclaimer is what makes every other case\n374-resolve as soon as a server is reachable. Neither is sufficient alone.\n375-\n376:### Deposit Admission\n377-\n378-The adapter runs in a callback context: it cannot wait, cannot park, and\n379-cannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the\n380-deposit destination must be one that always admits, and this is enforced\n381-where it can be — at assembly, against the destination's declared nature:\n382-\n383-Acceptance handoff media obey the same declared-admission rule: both media\n384-in every slot declare host-local reference admission, evict-oldest retention,\n385-capacity one, ability to carry their respective envelope (including the live\n386-handle in an offer), and a lifetime longer than the endpoint. Their additional\n387-one-outstanding-offer discipline — not a different retention mode — is what\n388-prevents capability loss.\n389-\n390-The acknowledgement's per-attachment traffic medium obeys the same rule and\n391-must outlive that attachment. Supplying it in the acknowledgement makes its\n392-cursor-before-delivery ordering explicit and isolates one attachment's payload\n393-retention pressure from every other attachment.\n394-\n395-The server boundary control medium also declares evict-oldest retention,\n396-capacity, `:portable-values`, and a lifetime longer than the endpoint. Its\n397-cursor is minted before listener start, as are the handoff cursors.\n398-\n399-- The destination's assembly declaration contains both **retention** and\n400-  **value domain**. Retention is evict-oldest with a host-declared capacity.\n401-  The value domain is either `:host-values`, which may retain host references,\n402-  or `:portable-values`, which admits exactly the boundary codec's structural\n403-  domain. A WebSocket traffic medium may use either; a handoff offer medium\n404-  must use `:host-values` because it carries a live handle. It never answers\n405-  `:dao.stream/full`; under\n406-  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s to lagging cursors — the one\n407-  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an\n408-  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the\n409-  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed\n410-  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting\n411-  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,\n412-  like any storage failure.)\n413-- The destination must also be able to **carry every envelope this transport\n414-  deposits** — attachment identifiers, lifecycle events, and every value the\n415-  codec admits. A destination that evicts oldest but answers\n416-  `:dao.stream/invalid-value` on some envelope is as unwired as a reject-mode\n417-  one; retention mode alone is not admission.\n418-- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused\n419-  when the boundary is composed. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before\n420-  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.\n421-- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit\n422-  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is\n423-  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination\n424-  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;\n425-  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary\n426-  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.\n427-  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the\n428-  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and\n429-  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary\n430-  `attach!`.\n431-\n432-Sizing the window is choosing how much cursor lag to tolerate before gaps\n433-appear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Whether\n434-that window can be resized after the fact is the destination transport's\n435-declared nature, not this transport's business. An adaptive policy — grow on\n436-rising gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own\n437-telemetry, not a transport behavior.\n438-\n439-The accounting is then honest, though not complete: declared retention\n440-pressure surfaces as `gap`s to cursors that span the eviction, and a dead\n441-destination tears the\n--\n587-  host-composed event medium, correlated by attachment identity. If releasing\n588-  the host socket fails after the local state transition, the local traffic\n589-  medium first receives\n590-  `{:ws/attachment <id> :ws/event :ws/error :ws/reason :ws/close-failure}`;\n591-  the later close-completion still contributes the single terminal lifecycle\n592:  event. A diagnostic deposit that itself fails follows Deposit Admission.\n593-- Connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited: the served stream\n594-  persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` with the same\n595-  descriptor rejoins the same stream. What a client must not confuse with it\n596-  is the served stream *ending*, which is a different fact reaching it by a\n597-  different route (see Ending a served stream).\n598-\n599-## Elements and Serialization\n600-\n601-The wire is a serialization boundary. Every element must survive the codec\n602-structurally unchanged; a handle can never travel — its portable\n603-descriptor can.\n604-\n605-The wire codec is the connection's codec profile (see The Handshake):\n606-**Transit JSON encoded as one UTF-8 WebSocket text message per value**, or\n607-**canonical CBOR encoded as one WebSocket binary message per value**. Every\n608-application value is framed as:\n609-\n610-```clojure\n611-{:ws/frame :ws/value\n612- :ws/value <value>}\n613-```\n614-\n615-The portable value domain is `nil`, booleans, strings, qualified or\n616-unqualified keywords and symbols, safe integers in\n617-`[-9007199254740991, 9007199254740991]`, finite doubles, vectors, lists,\n618-sets, and maps recursively composed from that domain. The Transit profile is\n619-exactly that domain — no custom handlers and no metadata participate. The\n620-CBOR profile is that domain widened with metadata — recursively portable\n621-metadata on collections and symbols, reader positions included — with host\n622-byte payloads (`byte[]` / `Uint8Array` / `Uint8List`) riding as CBOR byte\n623-strings (major type 2), the carrier `dao.jing.stream`'s boundary adapter\n624-puts Jing's canonical bytes on, and it\n625-keeps lists and vectors distinct: identifiers ride CBOR tag 39, sets ride\n626-tag 258, and metadata and list-hood ride tag 27 frames (`clojure/with-meta`\n627-and `dao.stream/list`). A sender unable to encode a value in its profile's\n628-domain returns `:dao.stream/invalid-value` and sends nothing. Across hosts,\n629-numeric structural equality is by mathematical value within this domain: an\n630-integral finite double and the equal safe integer are equivalent even if a\n631-host codec materializes different numeric classes.\n632-\n633-The CBOR wire is **canonical CBOR only, enforced on receive**: a receiver\n634-decodes, re-encodes, and byte-compares, so an indefinite length, a\n635-non-minimal integer, a non-shortest float, duplicate map keys, or map keys\n636-outside canonical bytewise order are protocol failures, not silently\n637-normalized input. The price is roughly one extra encode per inbound\n638-message; the return is a byte-exact wire across all three hosts and a\n639-duplicate-key guard no host codec can skip.\n640-\n641-All protocol keywords use exactly one namespace separator — for example\n642-`:ws/frame`, never a multi-slash spelling such as `:ws/wire/frame` — so the\n643-vocabulary reads on clj, cljs, and cljd.\n644-\n645-A message of the profile's wrong frame kind, a text message that is not\n646-valid Transit JSON, a binary payload that is not canonical CBOR inside the\n647-profile's portable domain, an unknown `:ws/frame` value, a missing required\n648-key, a value outside the portable domain, or a `:ws/value` frame received\n649-before `:ws/accept` has been sent is\n650-a protocol failure. Once an attachment identity exists, the\n651-receiver first deposits:\n652-\n653-```clojure\n654-{:ws/attachment <id>\n655- :ws/event      :ws/error\n656- :ws/reason     :ws/decode-failure}\n657-```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '76,270p'\"","aggregated_output":"    76\t## Authority\n    77\t\n    78\t- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n    79\t  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\n    80\t  evidence and counts as such.\n    81\t- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n    82\t  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n    83\t  measured as though it had not arrived.\n    84\t- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\n    85\t  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\n    86\t- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\n    87\t  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\n    88\t  nothing in this vocabulary.\n    89\t- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\n    90\t  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\n    91\t- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\n    92\t  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\n    93\t  ends the lease.\n    94\t\n    95\t## Prohibitions\n    96\t\n    97\t- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n    98\t  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n    99\t- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n   100\t- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n   101\t- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n   102\t- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n   103\t\n   104\t## Time\n   105\t\n   106\tTime reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\n   107\tclock. Both read a tick stream their composition wires. Readings on one stream\n   108\tnever decrease, and a composition persisting a ledger across a restart keeps\n   109\tits readings comparable across it.\n   110\t\n   111\tA tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\n   112\tunder *Judging with incomplete evidence*.\n   113\t\n   114\t## The judge\n   115\t\n   116\tThe judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\n   117\treclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\n   118\tresource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\n   119\tinterpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\n   120\tinterpreter performing its own act.\n   121\t\n   122\t**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n   123\t\n   124\t- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\n   125\t- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\n   126\t  changes;\n   127\t- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\n   128\t  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\n   129\t- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\n   130\t- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n   131\t  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n   132\t\n   133\tA lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\n   134\tand **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n   135\tappended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\n   138\tas a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\n   139\t\n   140\t### The pass\n   141\t\n   142\tA pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\n   143\tin this order:\n   144\t\n   145\t1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\n   146\t   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n   147\t   **now** for the whole pass.\n   148\t2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n   149\t   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\n   150\t   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n   151\t   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n   152\t   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n   153\t   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\n   154\t   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\n   155\t   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\n   156\t   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n   157\t   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n   158\t   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n   159\t3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n   160\t   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n   161\t   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n   162\t4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n   163\t   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n   164\t   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n   165\t   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n   166\t   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n   167\t   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n   168\t   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n   169\t   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\n   170\t   resumed reading.\n   171\t5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n   172\t   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n   173\t   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n   174\t   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n   175\t6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n   176\t   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n   177\t   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n   178\t   first clause of step 4.\n   179\t\n   180\tThe record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\n   181\tfact is never evidence of tenure.**\n   182\t\n   183\tA drain that ends in `:dao.stream/end` retires that cursor for subsequent\n   184\tpasses. A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/transport-error` ends the pass before\n   185\tstep 4.\n   186\t\n   187\t### Judging with incomplete evidence\n   188\t\n   189\tAbsence is evidence only over a window the judge observed. After a\n   190\t`:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is `unknown`,\n   191\tand a surviving older renewal is not inferred to be the newest. A lease\n   192\trecovered from a persisted ledger is `unknown`. Conditions other than\n   193\t`:silence` apply to an `unknown` lease unchanged.\n   194\t\n   195\t### Restart\n   196\t\n   197\tA grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\n   198\tresource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\n   199\tThe reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\n   200\t\n   201\tThis requires an inventory of possessed resources and a recoverable holder for\n   202\teach, both of which belong to the resource rather than to this vocabulary.\n   203\tA grantor with neither persists its ledger instead.\n   204\t\n   205\t## The holder\n   206\t\n   207\t- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n   208\t  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n   209\t  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n   210\t- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n   211\t  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n   212\t  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n   213\t- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n   214\t  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n   215\t  whether or not anything has been heard.\n   216\t- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n   217\t  it.\n   218\t- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n   219\t  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n   220\t  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n   221\t  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n   222\t\n   223\tFor cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\n   224\twhich it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n   225\tjudge's tenure start. The holder's observation-flight interval does not\n   226\textend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n   227\trequiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n   228\t\n   229\tThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n   230\tfrom the resource.\n   231\t\n   232\t## Composition duties\n   233\t\n   234\tA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n   235\t\n   236\t- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n   237\t  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n   238\t- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n   239\t- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n   240\t- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n   241\t  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n   242\t  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n   243\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   244\t  at assembly.\n   245\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n   246\t- **Over a durable ledger, a reclaim that only reports readiness.** The\n   247\t  revocation is the transaction that records `:lapsed` and its accompanying\n   248\t  facts; its commit is the linearization point. No state claiming a\n   249\t  successful revocation leaves the grantor's boundary before that commit. A\n   250\t  grantor stream that does not answer `:dao.stream/ok` leaves the lease\n   251\t  `pending` in the judge and the recorded state unchanged. A judge rebuilt\n   252\t  at restart drains its proposal media from their oldest anchor, so a\n   253\t  proposal drained but left unanswered before the restart is judged again.\n   254\t- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n   255\t  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n   256\t- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n   257\t  refused at assembly otherwise.\n   258\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   259\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   260\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n   261\t  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n   262\t\n   263\t## Sizing\n   264\t\n   265\tStated as relations; the values belong to compositions.\n   266\t\n   267\t- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.\n   268\t- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and the rate skew between\n   269\t  its tick stream and the holder's.\n   270\t- A medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag — cadence plus drain time.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
