{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,96p;259,280p;1005,1018p'\"","aggregated_output":"    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n   259\t### Envelopes\n   260\t\n   261\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   262\t\n   263\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   264\t  dispatch key.\n   265\t\n   266\tEvery other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace\n   267\t(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n   268\t— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its\n   269\town keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.\n   270\t\n   271\tThe **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n   272\treference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n   273\tIts properties are settled:\n   274\t\n   275\t- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch\n   276\t  key.\n   277\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   278\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   279\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints of the same owning\n   280\t  transport; their reachability data\n  1005\t  extension is reserved, and `dao.stream.waitset.implementation-plan.md` pays\n  1006\t  the cost in the runtime without it.\n  1007\t- **Granularity.** An element is a whole value; there is no byte offset within\n  1008\t  one and no chunked element. Large media travel as content addresses in the\n  1009\t  payload, resolved through `dao.jing`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '91,177p;576,667p'\"","aggregated_output":"    91\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    92\t\n    93\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n    94\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n    95\tthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n    96\t\n    97\t```clojure\n    98\t;; request\n    99\t{:dao.stream/identity        id\n   100\t :dao.stream.remote/op       op        ; :dao.stream/descriptor |\n   101\t                                       ; :dao.stream/cursor |\n   102\t                                       ; :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n   103\t :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n   104\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel\n   105\t                                       ; reader the asker holds\n   106\t :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n   107\t\n   108\t;; answer, the ordinary case: the contract's outcome map for the requested\n   109\t;; operation, verbatim, plus\n   110\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   111\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   112\t :dao.stream.remote/surface  S         ; descriptor answers only: the\n   113\t                                       ; entry's declared surface\n   114\t :dao.stream.remote/more     [...]}    ; optional, next only: further\n   115\t                                       ; outcome maps in order\n   116\t\n   117\t;; answer, a protocol error: one shape for every operation, a key instead of\n   118\t;; an outcome map, because no operation's outcome set can carry it\n   119\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   120\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   121\t :dao.stream.remote/error    e}        ; one of the three errors below\n   122\t\n   123\t;; named request: a name in place of the identity, descriptor only\n   124\t{:dao.stream.remote/name     nm\n   125\t :dao.stream.remote/op       :dao.stream/descriptor\n   126\t :dao.stream.remote/args     []\n   127\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n}\n   128\t\n   129\t;; its answers: the descriptor answer of the entry the name maps to, with\n   130\t;; that entry's own :dao.stream/identity and surface, plus\n   131\t{:dao.stream.remote/name     nm}\n   132\t;; or the not-found error, carrying the name and no identity\n   133\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   134\t :dao.stream.remote/name     nm\n   135\t :dao.stream.remote/error    :dao.stream.remote/not-found}\n   136\t```\n   137\t\n   138\tA request is one value on the channel writer; an answer is one value on the\n   139\tother end's. The request map is open: keys not named here pass to the mirror\n   140\tstep's middleware chain (section 7) and are otherwise ignored. A request that\n   141\tcarries `:dao.stream/identity` is an identity request whatever else it\n   142\tcarries, a `:dao.stream.remote/name` included, which is then ignored. A\n   143\trequest that carries `:dao.stream.remote/name` and no\n   144\t`:dao.stream/identity` is a named request; with any op other than\n   145\t`descriptor`, or with args other than `[]`, it is malformed. A value that\n   146\tis neither a well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as a\n   147\tdiagnostic below the mirror step and the link, as malformed wire input\n   148\t(`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries). Wire ops are exactly four: `close!`\n   149\tnever crosses, because the mirror holds nothing per remote party to close\n   150\tand the owner's close is never remote; `attach!` never crosses, because it\n   151\tconsumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n   152\t\n   153\t**Protocol errors.** The mirror answers three errors with the error key,\n   154\tnever by fabricating an outcome the operation's closed set forbids\n   155\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention; Surfaces):\n   156\t\n   157\t- `not-found`: the identity is absent from the table, whether never served,\n   158\t  retired by policy, or reclaimed with its lease. An identity this peer does\n   159\t  not serve has no operations and no surface, so this one error is the\n   160\t  honest answer for all four ops. For a named request it means the name is\n   161\t  absent from the name map or maps to an identity absent from the table,\n   162\t  and the answer carries the name and no identity.\n   163\t- `no-surface`: the op is outside the entry's declared surface, under the\n   164\t  fixed mapping: `cursor` and `next` need `:reader`, `append!` needs\n   165\t  `:writer`, `descriptor` needs no surface and is always answerable.\n   166\t- `oversize`: the channel writer refused the answer value itself\n   167\t  (`invalid-value`); the read is not skipped and no cursor advances past\n   168\t  it. Large media travel as `dao.jing` addresses (`dao.stream.md`,\n   169\t  Granularity). For a named request it carries the name, and an identity\n   170\t  only when the answer it replaces carried one.\n   171\t\n   172\tAll three names live under `:dao.stream.remote/`. The declared surface is\n   173\tanswer data, not descriptor data: the remote descriptor (2.2) carries no\n   174\tsurface, and the `descriptor` answer carries it under\n   175\t`:dao.stream.remote/surface`, the only place the surface is learned.\n   176\t\n   177\t### 2.2 The remote descriptor\n   576\t## 6. Lease integration\n   577\t\n   578\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   579\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   580\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires. What a lease governs:\n   581\t\n   582\t- A table entry a peer serves by its own policy: no lease; the peer retires\n   583\t  it when it likes.\n   584\t- A table entry served for a remote party (a stream a migrated continuation\n   585\t  still reads): yes; the remote party holds it, the holding peer grants and\n   586\t  judges, the identity is the subject.\n   587\t- A relay pair on a meeting peer: yes; the requesting peer holds it, the\n   588\t  meeting peer grants and judges, the two identities are the subject.\n   589\t- Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets: no; governed by close\n   590\t  or by a bounded buffer.\n   591\t- NAT mappings and punch state: no; possessed by the NAT box, so no judge\n   592\t  can exist, and keep-alive cadence is the peer's own (section 4).\n   593\t- Board postings: no; records under the board's retention, each carrying the\n   594\t  lease id of the registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a\n   595\t  posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.\n   596\t\n   597\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   598\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   599\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   600\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   601\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   602\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   603\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   604\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   605\tholder by the medium, the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` names as\n   606\tper-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The renewal\n   607\tmedium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes the\n   608\tsubject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and reports\n   609\tsuccess. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   610\t\n   611\tUntil ShiBi exists, a renewal medium is reachable by anyone who learns its\n   612\tdescriptor and accepts a third party's renewal, extending a lease it does\n   613\tnot hold: attribution needs no credential, extension may want one, and a\n   614\tcomposition that must not accept it composes a gate on the renewal entry.\n   615\t\n   616\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   617\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   618\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   619\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n   620\twriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n   621\t\n   622\t**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers the `not-found` protocol error for\n   623\ta reclaimed identity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder\n   624\tmay also read the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n   625\t`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\n   626\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   627\t\n   628\t**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers or\n   629\tthe channel's `end` (2.4); the table entry persists, so a holder that\n   630\treconnects within duration plus tolerance finds its entry, its cursors and\n   631\tits lease intact. Expiry is a present answer, the `not-found` error, on a\n   632\thealthy channel. Only expiry frees anything. A meeting peer that restarts\n   633\tloses its table and ledger, so every pair is gone, holders observe\n   634\t`not-found` and re-propose; this is `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant\n   635\twith an empty inventory, correct because the table is process-scoped.\n   636\t\n   637\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`.\n   638\t\n   639\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   640\t\n   641\tMiddleware preserves positions and outcome kinds as specified in\n   642\t`dao.stream.middleware.md`. Network attachment points are the table entry\n   643\t(gate, value encryption, redaction or metering), the reflection (credential\n   644\tpresentation, value decryption or metering), and the channel (reserved for\n   645\tdeferred channel-level wrapping).\n   646\t\n   647\tKeys and chains are composition data, never descriptor fields. Value-level\n   648\tencryption hides values only; identity, ops, cursors, sizes and timing stay\n   649\tvisible. Channel encryption is deferred: a relay sees every envelope and\n   650\tany value not hidden by middleware. A future channel wrapper must order\n   651\tcodec, encryption and fragmentation in that order. Key distribution is\n   652\tunspecified; use library ciphers.\n   653\t\n   654\tThe capability-agnostic seam is an opaque per-request credential key,\n   655\tmirror-supplied channel context, mirror-side gate and reflection-side\n   656\t`present`; refusal is `:dao.stream/refused`. No token or verification\n   657\tpolicy is specified. The gate consumes an index-published decision. The\n   658\tone ShiBi fit this version chooses: its index interpreter publishes the\n   659\ttuple space's current query value as the decision, and its query\n   660\tinterpreter is `verify`, a query over that value with the request as\n   661\tbindings, the shape `dao.space.query/q` has. `dao.shibi.md` states the\n   662\tadapter contract and its limits (one snapshot, bounded cost, no waiting);\n   663\trequest-specific decisions awaited over streams are a non-goal there.\n   664\t\n   665\tLease attribution uses per-author media; renewal gates are optional.\n   666\t\n   667\t## 8. Unification and contract boundary\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '205,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"   205\t## The holder\n   206\t\n   207\t- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n   208\t  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n   209\t  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n   210\t- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n   211\t  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n   212\t  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n   213\t- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n   214\t  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n   215\t  whether or not anything has been heard.\n   216\t- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n   217\t  it.\n   218\t- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n   219\t  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n   220\t  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n   221\t  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n   222\t\n   223\tFor cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\n   224\twhich it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n   225\tjudge's tenure start. The holder's observation-flight interval does not\n   226\textend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n   227\trequiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n   228\t\n   229\tThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n   230\tfrom the resource.\n   231\t\n   232\t## Composition duties\n   233\t\n   234\tA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n   235\t\n   236\t- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n   237\t  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n   238\t- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n   239\t- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n   240\t- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n   241\t  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n   242\t  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n   243\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   244\t  at assembly.\n   245\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n   246\t- **Over a durable ledger, a reclaim that only reports readiness.** The\n   247\t  revocation is the transaction that records `:lapsed` and its accompanying\n   248\t  facts; its commit is the linearization point. No state claiming a\n   249\t  successful revocation leaves the grantor's boundary before that commit. A\n   250\t  grantor stream that does not answer `:dao.stream/ok` leaves the lease\n   251\t  `pending` in the judge and the recorded state unchanged. A judge rebuilt\n   252\t  at restart drains its proposal media from their oldest anchor, so a\n   253\t  proposal drained but left unanswered before the restart is judged again.\n   254\t- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n   255\t  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n   256\t- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n   257\t  refused at assembly otherwise.\n   258\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   259\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   260\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
