{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '405,478p'\"","aggregated_output":"   405\t## Surfaces\n   406\t\n   407\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   408\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   409\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   410\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   411\t\n   412\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   413\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   414\tAn operation a handle does not\n   415\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   416\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   417\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   418\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   419\toperation exists.\n   420\t\n   421\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   422\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   423\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   424\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   425\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   426\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   427\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   428\t\n   429\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   430\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   431\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   432\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   433\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   434\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   435\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   436\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   437\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   438\t\n   439\tA handle may be composed over another handle on the same logical stream: it\n   440\tdeclares that stream's identity, implements no surface the inner handle\n   441\tlacks, passes cursors through unchanged, and may present values under its\n   442\town interpretation. Such a handle is host-composed, like a transport\n   443\tconstructor's closures, and is otherwise an ordinary handle.\n   444\t\n   445\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   446\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   447\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   448\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   449\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   450\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   451\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   452\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   453\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   454\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   455\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   456\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   457\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   458\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   459\t\n   460\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   461\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   462\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   463\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   464\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   465\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n   466\trecord of its own departure has nowhere to land, and the failure is observable\n   467\tonly to whoever holds the deposit result and through subsequent operations on\n   468\tthe handle.\n   469\t\n   470\tA transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n   471\texcludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n   472\timpossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n   473\treports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n   474\tevicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a `gap` to a cursor that\n   475\tspans the eviction, and never as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n   476\tthat cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n   477\texclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n   478\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
