{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '1655,1701p;2160,2220p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1655\t### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\n  1656\t\n  1657\t`dao.lease.md` is explicit that a judge must be composed **inside the\n  1658\tboundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\n  1659\tit itself holds*; a judge that possesses nothing is outside the contract.\n  1660\tThe first draft designated \"the composition-designated arbiter for the\n  1661\tcarrier medium,\" defaulting to the emitter — an arbiter relative to a\n  1662\tcarrier, possessing an offer, which is a copy of content: the same\n  1663\toccurrence carried on a second medium would acquire a second arbiter, and\n  1664\tneither arbiter controls execution of anything. This section replaces that\n  1665\twith a grounded design.\n  1666\t\n  1667\t**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\n  1668\tdatoms about one occurrence id on the arbitration `dao.space` — its offer,\n  1669\tgrants, renewals, lapses, and resumed reports, plus the occurrence's\n  1670\t**admission epoch** (§7.7.5). The **grantor is the boundary that transacts\n  1671\tthat space** — its transactor. Possession is exact and of the right kind:\n  1672\t\n  1673\t- the ledger's datom base lives inside the transactor's boundary, and\n  1674\t  admission to it is the transactor's alone — no other boundary can write\n  1675\t  those datoms, which is why grantor-authored facts are worth more than\n  1676\t  holder reports;\n  1677\t- the **reclaim** is the grantor acting on what it holds: recording the\n  1678\t  lapse on its own base and advancing the occurrence's epoch datom. It\n  1679\t  needs no cooperation from any holder and no reach into any carrier;\n  1680\t- the arbiter is **not medium-relative**: the binding\n  1681\t  occurrence → arbitration space is minted at first export and travels *in\n  1682\t  the value* (`:yin.k/arbitration`, §7.2), so every carrier, every snapshot\n  1683\t  variant, and every copy names the same one authority. The same content on\n  1684\t  two media does not create a second arbiter, because the authority is named\n  1685\t  by the occurrence, not discovered from wherever a value happened to land;\n  1686\t- **one holder per occurrence is the grantor's policy, enforced where\n  1687\t  policy lives**: `dao.lease` does not impose it, and UCF does not add to\n  1688\t  the vocabulary — the grantor's ledger admits at most one live lease per\n  1689\t  occurrence (a second grant requires the prior lease reclaimed first,\n  1690\t  which `dao.lease.md`'s own validity rules make the grantor the sole\n  1691\t  author of). Exclusive custody is thus a property of the ledger the\n  1692\t  grantor possesses, not an aspiration over streams it does not.\n  1693\t\n  1694\tWhat is possessed, concretely, is the *right to admit effects as this\n  1695\toccurrence* — the ledger is where epochs and dedup-stamped admissions are\n  1696\trecorded, which is what §7.7.5's fencing consults. A composition that\n  1697\tcannot wire a `dao.space` transactor as authority cannot offer\n  1698\t`:yin.k/exclusive` custody at all; it composes `:yin.k/fork` only. That is\n  1699\tan honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over.\n  1700\t\n  1701\t### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\n  2160\t  epoch current at that grant, so the first grant binds 0 and the\n  2161\t  grant after `k` reclaims binds `k`. A successor is a new\n  2162\t  occurrence and starts at 0.\n  2163\t- (M-next C, slice C6.) **The epoch change** is the grantor's fact\n  2164\t\n  2165\t  ```clojure\n  2166\t  {:yin.k/custody    :yin.k/reclaimed\n  2167\t   :yin.k/occurrence O\n  2168\t   :dao.lease/lease  L\n  2169\t   :yin.k/epoch      e}             ; the epoch after the reclaim\n  2170\t  ```\n  2171\t\n  2172\t  committed in the transaction that records `L`'s\n  2173\t  `:dao.lease/lapsed` fact. Every lapse has exactly one, and the\n  2174\t  ledger refuses a lapse without it or it without its lapse. At the\n  2175\t  bound it is still written, carrying the unchanged epoch.\n  2176\t- (M-next C, slice C6.) **A refusal** is a pair committed in one\n  2177\t  transaction: the plain `:dao.lease/rejected` lease fact, verbatim\n  2178\t  `dao.lease` (it names no proposer), and the grantor's\n  2179\t\n  2180\t  ```clojure\n  2181\t  {:yin.k/custody      :yin.k/refused\n  2182\t   :yin.k/proposer     P\n  2183\t   :dao.lease/proposal pid}\n  2184\t  ```\n  2185\t\n  2186\t  A lease judge switches on the lease keys and ignores the refused\n  2187\t  fact; a reader learns which proposal a refusal answered, keyed\n  2188\t  `[proposer proposal-id]`, from the refused fact alone. The ledger\n  2189\t  refuses either half\n  2190\t  without the other. A proposal id is answered at most once, so a\n  2191\t  candidate refused, or answered `:yin.k/not-holder` (7.8), proposes\n  2192\t  again with a fresh proposal id.\n  2193\t- **Restart.** The epoch never decreases, is never reset, and is\n  2194\t  never reused. A grantor that restarts recovers the epoch from its\n  2195\t  durable ledger and reclaims, which raises it, before it grants\n  2196\t  (7.7.7). A grantor that cannot recover the epoch grants nothing\n  2197\t  for that occurrence: 7.7.7's reclaim-and-regrant presumes the\n  2198\t  epoch survived. A new authority never starts an old occurrence at\n  2199\t  0. Recovery from lost epoch or dedup state is governance, outside\n  2200\t  this protocol (7.7.5). (M-next C, slice C6.) Reopen runs 7.7.7's\n  2201\t  steps, rebuilding the judge after the reclaims: a reclaim that does\n  2202\t  not commit refuses the open, and reopen is retried.\n  2203\t- **Exhaustion.** Epoch 2^52-1 is usable until a reclaim would\n  2204\t  increment it: a grant bound at that value is valid, and its\n  2205\t  holder's effects are admitted like any other's. That reclaim ends\n  2206\t  the tenure, leaves the epoch unchanged, and permanently exhausts\n  2207\t  the occurrence. Thereafter admission returns `:suspended` before\n  2208\t  tenure checking, and no effect commits. Exhaustion is derived\n  2209\t  from the ledger: a lapse recorded for the lease bound at 2^52-1.\n  2210\t  An exhausted tenure cannot produce an eligible exclusive\n  2211\t  successor: the authority accepts no completion and grants nothing\n  2212\t  for the occurrence. Carrier bytes may remain as history, but a\n  2213\t  publication cannot reopen custody. (M-next C, slices C6 and C8.)\n  2214\t  In the ledger, that lapse's epoch change carries the lease's own\n  2215\t  binding epoch, which only a lease bound at 2^52-1 can do; that is\n  2216\t  the exhaustion. A report recorded at the bound is evidence: its\n  2217\t  release exhausts the occurrence without completing it.\n  2218\t- **Why a binding.** A remote holder cannot infer authoritative\n  2219\t  tenure from what it observes: `:dao.lease/lapsed` does not cross\n  2220\t  to it (`dao.lease.md`, *Carriage*), and its view of the ledger is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
