{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '258,300p'; rg -n 'fetch.*clock|clock.*fetch|clock-free' docs/design\"","aggregated_output":"   258\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   259\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   260\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n   261\t  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n   262\t\n   263\t## Sizing\n   264\t\n   265\tStated as relations; the values belong to compositions.\n   266\t\n   267\t- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.\n   268\t- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and the rate skew between\n   269\t  its tick stream and the holder's.\n   270\t- A medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag — cadence plus drain time.\n   271\t- A cap below the duration is permitted, and tenure then ends at the cap.\n   272\t- The holder's renewal interval plus one period of its tick stream is\n   273\t  strictly below half the granted duration: a renewal lands at the first\n   274\t  reading at or past the interval, up to one period late, so the\n   275\t  before-half guarantee is bought against interval plus period.\n   276\t\n   277\t## Limits\n   278\t\n   279\t- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\n   280\t  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n   281\t  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\n   282\t  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n   283\t  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\n   284\t- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n   285\t- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\n   286\t  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\n   287\t  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\n   288\t- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\n   289\t  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n   290\t\n   291\t## Carriage\n   292\t\n   293\tProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n   294\tpayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n   295\tis delivery, not a second authoring.\n   296\t\n   297\t`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n   298\tremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n   299\tordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\n   300\t`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:921:linker stays clock-free.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2371:  (section 6.3). M5 shipped a clock-free pending run with `(abandon)`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:74:  `dao.lease`; the linker is clock-free** (owner, 2026-09-26). No rule\ndocs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md:4:pending-link failure policy -- the clock-free linker stands, the\ndocs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md:31:- `fetch` takes no deadline. The linker and the engine stay clock-free.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"agent_message","text":"The main defects are the distinction between SSE subscription and user input, stale-token routing after reclaim, and recovery when ingress history is evicted. Both HTTP example blocks also fail EDN parsing. Q6’s carrier choice is reasonable, but it still needs an explicit content-store fetch path and access policy; an address alone supplies neither."}}
