{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.md | sed -n '319,345p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md | sed -n '25,38p'\"","aggregated_output":"   319\t## Reads\n   320\t\n   321\tThe storage read operation resolves a strict content address to the exact\n   322\topaque value stored at that address. `dao.jing/get` accepts only\n   323\tregistered `:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>` addresses; arbitrary keys and mutable\n   324\troots are outside DaoJing and throw before a backend is consulted.\n   325\t\n   326\tA reader may access the target locally or through a remote transport\n   327\t(`dao.jing.content` over `dao.stream.remote.md`, which deprecates\n   328\t`dao.jing.remote`; `dao.jing.dht`). Location changes how bytes are obtained,\n   329\tnot how their identity or meaning is determined.\n   330\t\n   331\tHigher layers expose the semantic compositions the stream participates in. A\n   332\tquery reads a published index descriptor containing a serializable\n   333\tcontent-store coordinate plus an immutable manifest address.\n   334\t`dao.jing.coordinate/open!` interprets coordinates such as\n   335\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file :path ...}` or\n   336\t`{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests ... :dao.jing/answers ...}`\n   337\t(two remote descriptors, `dao.stream.remote.md` section 8; today only the\n   338\tdeprecated JVM form `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :url ...}` exists)\n   339\tinto local handles;\n   340\tunsupported coordinates fail closed. These coordinates are caller-supplied\n   341\tvalues, not state inferred from storage. DaoJing does not infer a source or\n   342\tcoordinate from the stream that carried a payload, and no content address is\n   343\tever treated as a mutable root.\n   344\t\n   345\t## Cursor tracking and recovery\n    25\tRetry cadence, deadline and permanent absence are the composition's\n    26\t(`yin.vm.linker.md` D6 and section 6.3), so the policy belongs to\n    27\t`yin.repl`, and the owner asked that it be configurable.\n    28\t\n    29\t## 2. Rulings that bound the design\n    30\t\n    31\t- `fetch` takes no deadline. The linker and the engine stay clock-free.\n    32\t  Time reaches a policy only as data the composition supplies.\n    33\t- `require` stays an ordinary function. A deadline is a session setting,\n    34\t  not an argument of `require` and not a new special form.\n    35\t- The M5 pending run, `(abandon)`, the retained-line replay and the\n    36\t  identity carry across rollbacks are unchanged. A policy adds one more\n    37\t  way to reach the abandon path; it adds no second path.\n    38\t- Rule R and the sealed private resources stand: a policy is composition\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-07 06:17:09 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 13:17:09 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a11500-e74e-7aa2-a0d7-7a8a9561d334\n\nReview complete. Files unchanged. The owner decisions are represented, but Mode 1 has unresolved lifecycle defects.\n\n| Severity | File:line | Invariant/evidence | Recommended correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **High** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:39` | The initial SSE GET carries the occurrence token. Lines 31–55 route every token-bearing request to the continuation and consume one input. Thus opening the subscription advances the application; reconnecting with its original URL subsequently becomes stale and ends under line 57. | Specify subscription establishment and reconnection as distinct interpreted request kinds that do not consume application input. Preserve token-only admission. |\n| **High** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:71` | Consumption releases the parked lease; reclaim retracts its record. Line 39 treats a token naming no live occurrence as a fresh session, while line 57 requires consumed tokens to re-project the current occurrence. The required predecessor-to-current routing survives nowhere explicitly. | Define retained token lineage and terminal/expired states. Consumed tokens must resolve to the current occurrence until an explicitly declared expiry. |\n| **High** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:55` | Open exchanges are “derived from ingress events, not kept,” but HTTP ingress expressly evicts history (`dao.stream.http.md:59`). Once an opening event is evicted, replay cannot reconstruct a still-open exchange. A judge missing that request may also never grant its lease. | Specify ingress-gap recovery, retention requirements, and reconciliation against the boundary’s owned exchange inventory. Define the retained projection needed by responders. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:71` | `dao.lease.md:101` says reclaim frees the resource, never the record. “Reclaim retracts the parked record” does not distinguish executable resources from immutable history. | Reclaim the runnable parked resource and append its retirement fact; explicitly preserve historical continuation and lease records. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:71` | The Architect’s holder choice is permitted, but its attribution is unspecified. `dao.lease.md:81–83,240–244` requires attributable holder renewals and refuses assembly without a compatible resolver. “Session agent” supplies neither its data identity nor its renewal medium. | Name the holder’s plain-data identity, attributed renewal/release medium, grant observation path, and holder-bound discipline. Clarify that connection-based renewal deliberately retains connected but unused sessions indefinitely. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.star.md:93` | Q6 names a body address and arbitration/channel descriptors, but no content-store coordinate or request/answer convention. `dao.jing.md:326–342` requires explicit location data; `dao.stream.remote.md:148–151` defines four stream operations, with no content-address fetch operation. | Specify the composed `dao.jing.content` fetch path, publication-before-notice ordering, integrity verification, access gate, and loss/absence outcomes. Keep fetch clock-free. |\n| **Medium** | `docs/design/dao.stream.http.md:85` | A terminal event cannot establish which individual effects were performed. Some chunks may succeed before a failed write; effects can also have been appended before the terminal event and processed afterward. The claimed “precedes any effect … ignored” ordering is undefined across two media. Line 88’s “anything after end … closes” also conflicts with ignoring effects for non-open exchanges. | State that terminal events report exchange termination without per-effect delivery guarantees. Define post-end effects as ignored and scope ordering to each medium. |\n| **Low** | `docs/design/dao.stream.http.md:43` | Both fenced examples fail `clojure.edn/read`: **“Map literal must contain an even number of forms.”** The header maps contain standalone ellipsis symbols. | Replace ellipses with concrete key/value pairs. Use literal portable body examples, or label schematic placeholders explicitly. |\n| **Low** | `docs/design/dao.stream.http.md:8` | The document literally names Datastar, SSE, and `yin.vm`. The Architect’s report that it names none is false. This sentence creates no actual dependency, however. | Replace it with “This document is application-agnostic.” |\n\nQ6 is **architecturally reasonable, but incomplete**. Sending a notice through the patch and fetching canonical content through an existing channel fits the stream and UCF designs. Moving the body off shared egress reduces direct disclosure there; it provides confidentiality only if content fetching is gated. A readable address is not an authorization mechanism.\n\nChecks that passed:\n\n- **Owner decisions:** Mode 2 waits for UCF v1; migration back is allow-listed pending confinement; stale input defaults to one-shot re-projection; parked expiry uses `dao.lease`; occurrence tokens are the sole Mode 1 gate; Q6 is expressly unconfirmed. Q4’s holder choice is correctly identified as the Architect’s design.\n- **Six foundational invariants:** No additional direct violation found beyond the lifecycle and explicit-state gaps above. Polling, stream deposits, and interpretation boundaries are generally explicit.\n- **WebSocket deposit admission:** HTTP matches the declared evict-oldest retention, capacity, portable-domain admission, lifetime, assembly rejection, and non-`ok` teardown rules.\n- **UCF and remote access:** Export fencing, grant-before-resume, isolated lowering, effect fencing, and channel-loss versus reclaimed-entry distinctions agree with the referenced designs.\n- **P2P and clock-free fetch:** No privileged node kind or fetch deadline is introduced.\n- **References:** All explicit Markdown targets exist; the cited UCF sections and remote sections exist.\n- **Published blog:** No direct contradiction found. Durable “paused for weeks” operation remains an acknowledged composition gap, rather than a demonstrated capability of the process-scoped design."}}
